window.harnessChunks["rows-9d11d44aacdc2cc6.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T18:45:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/patriceckhart/zot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zot</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/patriceckhart/zot/tree/2a19df15ba8278dd6a92240ab6c2ae267c3cf6c3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2a19df15ba8278dd6a92240ab6c2ae267c3cf6c3\">2a19df1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out open-ended coding and inspection by choosing local file/tool/shell actions with model-to-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repository state, edit/test consequences, command failures, multiple file/tool observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool and coding action based on the current messages and results. - Decision owner: first-party Zot core.Agent model-directed actor; external model endpoint supplies inference only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent model request, registered tools, native tool executor, first-party session state and credentials/provider adapters. - Closure path: task→oneTurn model tool calls→executeTools results appended to transcript→next runLoop iteration or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: core.Agent.runLoop is the shipped binary&#39;s ordinary interaction loop, and optional swarm child processes invoke their own first-party Zot agent loop, not an imported coding-agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: meaningful next task actions are model-directed rather than a fixed scripted series; runtime and human constraints bound these choices. - Evidence: packages/core/agent.go, packages/agent/swarmagent.go, packages/agent/modes/print.go. - Basis: explicit native source code. - Confidence: high for first-party path and feedback; no live model execution performed. - Caveats: performance and tool grants depend on model provider and operator configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination relation that diagnoses and attenuates concrete interference among separate swarm S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: swarm children share a writable workspace and may collide on edits; inter-worker write interference is a plausible disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate competing writes/task claims, return a collision finding or negotiated rescope to affected workers. No such native relation is established. - Decision owner: swarm management spawns independent child loops; parent can manually message children but no native inter-worker write-claim controller is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status/inbox and shared workspace; these are generic routing/status, not a conflict lock/arbiter. - Closure path: child completion returns a summary to parent, not a conflict-specific arbitration-and-correction cycle among worker S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent parallel work and communication do not establish S2 without a returned response to specific inter-S1 disturbance. README explicitly cautions that children edit the same files without isolation. - Evidence: README.md, packages/agent/swarm/swarm.go, packages/agent/modes/interactive.go, packages/agent/tools/swarmspawn.go. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: medium-high, at default shared-workspace swarm boundary. - Caveats: user-created external Git worktrees might attenuate conflicts but cannot be credited as shipped first-party automatic S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent-operator whole-swarm current supervision of worker commitments and allocation while a coding workspace is active. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale/failed/background child activity, task assignment and progress across multiple running Zot child agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: view the swarm worker population and make actual current commitments by spawning another worker, redirecting work with a message, canceling/terminating work, or resuming a stopped worker. - Decision owner: human terminal operator through /swarm dashboard; the coding model&#39;s optional spawn tool is ordinary task delegation and does not prove a distinct autonomous S3 supervisor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: swarm SnapshotAll/current status, per-agent subprocess and inbox, session-scoped dashboard, SpawnReq, Stop, SendInput, Resume and child persistent state. - Closure path: whole-swarm dashboard status/activity view → operator&#39;s current worker assignment/intervention → first-party process spawn/send/stop/resume → live child run/inbox changes → updated statuses and outputs. - Boundary reachability: installed /swarm UI is a shipped optional interactive feature controlling first-party child Zot processes, rather than an external management application or developer-only test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decisive group-level current control belongs to the parent operator, hence P; auto-swarm alone is insufficient for autonomous S3. - Evidence: README.md, packages/agent/swarm/swarm.go, packages/agent/modes/swarmdialog.go, packages/agent/modes/interactive.go. - Whole-system current view: session-scoped swarm dashboard shows each child state, recent activity, error, task and lifecycle. - Current-control decision scope: operator changes which subordinate agents are running/paused, their task messages and active commitments from that group view. - Basis: first-party parent current supervision path. - Confidence: moderate for explicitly enabled operator swarm mode. - Caveats: S3 is not credited merely to auto-swarm summary or one ordinary coding session; no S3=A/C independent actor mode is established.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently owned complementary audit/verification function with a separate findings return and corrective disposition is shipped at this session/swarm boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous outputs, incomplete work or unverified code changes may occur, but review ability through ordinary model tools or optional user-installed skills is not S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect S1 claims/evidence and return findings into accountable current control; such a first-party distinct review path was not found. - Decision owner: one main coding model or manually spawned subagents under operator direction; no designated native auditor role/verdict authority mandatory for a specified supported mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic tool results, session logs, statuses and auto-swarm summary, without binding independent claim audit. - Closure path: generic child task report→parent summary only. No first-party independent audit claim→source access→finding→correction/stop gate beyond ordinary work completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: built-in children and a user-created reviewer prompt are generic S1/parent delegation, and optional extensions are not first-party audit owners. - Evidence: packages/core/agent.go, packages/agent/swarm/swarm.go, packages/agent/modes/interactive.go, packages/agent/tools/swarmspawn.go. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate, at inspected first-party standard distribution. - Caveats: user may spawn reviewer-like child tasks, but this is an external constructed audit; optional third-party extensions not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future/environment monitoring and owned prospective capability change loop at installed session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: context pressure and recurring user tasks are handled by compaction/persistent instructions; not a distinct adaptive external-intelligence remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a future-facing change to the system&#39;s capability from environmental evidence and return it to present operation; absent. - Decision owner: operator can modify settings/skills, and main model can re-plan current tasks; neither qualifies without outside-and-then strategy closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session compaction, per-project AGENTS.md, skills, extension loader, model selection, persistence. - Closure path: ongoing task context restored or compacted; no evidence-first adaptation option→binding prospective system change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, model switching and user skill installation are configuration/continuity mechanisms rather than S4 intelligence adaptation. - Evidence: packages/core/compact.go, packages/agent/skillpins.go, packages/agent/extcmd.go, packages/agent/settingsstore.go. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: external operator workflows can evolve installed Zot, outside credited first-party S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate organizational identity/ultimate-policy decision loop at this local coding/swam boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool approvals and provider credentials protect local execution, without adjudicating ultimate organizational purpose or policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue a binding ultimate identity/policy interpretation and return it to current/swarm operations; not evidenced. - Decision owner: human configures permissions, credentials and agent identity; runtime applies configured access bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent options, user tool confirmation, jail settings and extension policies. - Closure path: permission allows/denies a tool and config changes future tool availability, but neither establishes first-party rightful S5 identity authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent S3 operational control over child sessions does not automatically amount to ultimate organizational policy and purpose adjudication. - Evidence: packages/agent/config.go, packages/core/confirm.go, packages/agent/cli.go. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: external user or organization may provide a parent S5, not credited at this installed binary recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d9c402e6ce327f8e8c3afaa6837bcbd50b3e832/assessments/zot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d9c402e6ce327f8e8c3afaa6837bcbd50b3e832\">2d9c402 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T20:46:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/folke/zaly\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">zaly</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/folke/zaly/tree/a568c033de6418e5a24193f41ae8335ce0ddb2b2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a568c033de6418e5a24193f41ae8335ce0ddb2b2\">a568c03 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform a user coding request through model-selected inspection, edit, shell and related first-party tools, adapting steps to returned observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository organization, user implementation requirements, tool failures, slow process output and session context limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured language model selects task-specific tool calls and continuation after returned tool results, while permission checks may reject certain actions. - Decision owner: First-party Agent per-session model loop with configured provider; Tasks/tool handlers carry out those decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Streaming model adapter, default bash/edit/read/write/fetch/find/grep tools, tool result/session injection, background task poll/heartbeat, session JSONL replay, compaction/masking and permissions. - Closure path: User input → model tool selection → typed tool dispatch → execution result or permission denial → next model step, explicit stop or later task completion injected into session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses specific coding interventions and follow-up actions; fixed tools, prompts and permission presets constrain but do not replace that operative decision. - Boundary reachability: Default CLI loads createAgent and sets tools.active from user/project config defaults; its selected tool handlers execute through the same agent&#39;s step loop. - Evidence: standard CLI agent construction, model stream and step, run continuation, background results. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for first-party model/action wiring; no empirical live completion claim. - Caveats: Tool safety and task success depend on selected permission preset, provider availability and actual host behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Dampen real interference among distinct independently viable operational S1 cells and return coordination adjustments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential file/edit collisions if multiple coding models execute in the same workspace; simultaneous tool calls and task result ordering in one coding loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The default CLI excludes both named swarm agent-spawn/message tools and one-shot subagent; no installed inter-S1 conflict arbitration is reachable in that mode. - Decision owner: None established for a qualifying S2. Tasks sequences parallel/nonparallel tool calls and the optional Swarm transports messages when separately activated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-tool parallel flags, task chain ordering, user permissions, freshness checks, async completion injection and unselected SDK child/swarm APIs. - Closure path: Concurrent tools → deterministic task queuing or result notification → same S1 model; no inter-S1 conflict → coordination decision → independently adjusted S1 behavior closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, lifecycle and tool sequencing are not a dedicated disturbance-regulation function among operating cells; disabled subagent tools cannot be credited as shipped CLI inter-agent control. - Evidence: default tools explicitly omit agents, CLI loads active defaults, task call sequencing, unselected swarm implementation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high on default tool exposure, medium-high for bounded S2 classification. - Caveats: An opt-in consumer of the SDK may create operating subagents; that other supported mode needs independent evidence of concrete cross-agent interference and adjustment before grading S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Control current whole-system commitments, resources and priorities across operational divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task queue congestion, simultaneous tool usage, session/context budget and pending work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independent whole-current resource/commitment controller is evidenced; the parent coding model chooses individual tasks and users choose model/configuration settings. - Decision owner: No S3-specific owner in the default single-agent CLI. Tasks implements deterministic per-session pending/run/completion statuses. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Background tasks, task list/poll/stop, session statuses, token accounting, compaction and permission limits. - Closure path: Task submitted → tool registry executes/queues → result enters one model&#39;s context; not a managerial decision over independently operating S1 commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task scheduler mechanics and user controls cannot substitute for whole-current cross-cell decision authority. - Evidence: default single root, task manager, CLI user model selection. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The SDK can be embedded in a larger manager; no such separate current-control loop is credited at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently verify current-management operating claims against alternative operational reality and return corrective accountability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading task completion, unsafe edits, stale file assumptions and inaccurate run history. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party independent auditor of S3&#39;s current-management claim is wired; source tests, permissions and source-read freshness are task-local correctness/support. - Decision owner: None for S3; human project reviewers and test suites outside the running agent are adjacent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool result logs, session/message tree, edit freshness checks, activity status and tests in the developer repository. - Closure path: Tool/file observation → same operating model or human approval; no S3 → independent alternative reality audit → corrected S3 decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Inspecting output and enforcing write preconditions increases task reliability but does not create complementary management-audit authority. - Evidence: message/session state, file edit freshness and preflight, default tools. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An external human or plugin could perform review but is not a packaged S3 operating channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Observe and model prospective external change, choose adaptation strategies and return new capability commitments through current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project requirements, model-provider capabilities and future workflows could matter to the coding tool. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user may change provider/models/tools/resources; the runtime masks or compacts local history. No installed autonomous prospective external-environment options/adoption decision is shown. - Decision owner: Human and configuration/resource manager for capability selection, not a first-party S4 strategist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider registry and model switch, skills/plugins, resource pack reload, local token/compaction/masking and user commands. - Closure path: Current context pressure or user settings → changed local prompt/resources. No external future → evaluated strategic alternative → binding S3 operating return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hackable plugins, resource loaders and provider choice give extensibility, not agent-owned proactive adaptation to a forecast external environment. - Evidence: resource and model customization, CLI resource load/config, context masking/compaction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A custom skill could research future change, but that user-installed or task-instructed extension is a different candidate path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimately settle ultimate identity and governing values for the coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dangerous shell commands, editing without authorization, untrusted project instructions and conflicting user preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human chooses requested work, tool permissions and default presets. The runtime applies allow/ask/deny and user prompts; it has no legitimate independent final-purpose/policy decision. - Decision owner: Human operator/project configuration, mediated by deterministic PermissionManager and tool-specific gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission presets strict, readonly, permissive, yolo, per-tool allow/ask/deny rules and user approval callback. - Closure path: User/host policy → tool execution preflight → allow, ask or deny local coding action. It is enforcement, not agent-originated ultimate governance with return to S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Having a default prompt that calls the agent adaptive or a rule engine controlling tools does not authorize redefinition of the organization&#39;s identity and values. - Evidence: CLI permissions configuration, PermissionManager and presets, operator warning. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high about preset/human authority; medium-high about S5 absence. - Caveats: The explicitly permissive yolo mode changes protections, not VSM S5 decision ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a2fbb89ca6542b43c9b27861ad875883cd566ea0/assessments/za/zaly.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a2fbb89ca6542b43c9b27861ad875883cd566ea0\">a2fbb89 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:20:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Browser Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness/tree/afbcc381b963040c19627d788e40c7e7663171ee\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision afbcc381b963040c19627d788e40c7e7663171ee\">afbcc38 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: autonomous interactive browser task execution in the live web environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: page state, navigation, DOM/accessibility structure, authentication/session state, dynamic UI and task-specific browser mechanics. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven agent chooses browser actions, inspections, retries and task-specific helper additions. - Decision owner: autonomous agent actor operating through the first-party skill/control surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CDP helper layer, daemon, tab state, MCP/CLI transport, diagnostics/recovery and editable helper workspace. - Closure path: browser actions change page/environment state; subsequent observations and agent decisions consume those results until the task outcome is reached. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C. - Distinct S1 units: multiple agent actors may concurrently perform browser work through the same local Chrome/harness boundary. - Specific interference: one default daemon has one mutable attached/current tab; simultaneous tab switching/actions can race, causing an agent to act on or capture another agent&#39;s tab. - Coordination relation: the shipped skill requires browser operations through the default local daemon to be serialized; for true simultaneity it directs each task to an isolated remote browser, with a named local daemon only as a last-resort isolation mechanism. - Closure path: the chosen serialization/isolation arrangement changes later S1 browser behaviour by preventing concurrent mutation of the shared lane or separating the lanes entirely. - Ownership: Browser Harness supplies the S2-specific disturbance model and regulation protocol, but it does not itself establish a single autonomous first-party coordinator that chooses ordering/isolation across arbitrary external hosts. The path therefore requires composition with the host/orchestrator/participating agents. - Why not generic routing: the evidence identifies a concrete mutable-tab collision and a regulation response designed specifically to attenuate it. - Basis /\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The daemon manages connection/tab state and remote-browser lifecycle, but no distinct actor is established with a whole-system current view and discretionary authority over organization-wide resources, priorities or commitments. Connection recovery and lifecycle enforcement are supporting runtime mechanisms rather than S3 ownership. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - Recordings, screenshots, diagnostics and verification steps improve observability and task execution but do not form an independent complementary audit channel with separate judgment and corrective return into current control. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Agent-written reusable helpers can improve future task capability, but they arise from current task mechanics and are generated by the operating agent. The pinned distribution does not establish an externally/prospectively oriented environmental-intelligence loop that develops adaptation options and closes them into present capability through S3. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Skill rules, browser permission prompts and user choices such as recording/cloud usage constrain operation but do not establish an identity/ultimate-policy decision loop at the harness recursion. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029/assessments/browser-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029\">96a7716 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T14:57:00Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/surefire-ai/korus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Korus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/surefire-ai/korus/tree/ed5ea6a9fca86f43df161a100e913c29fa9b40c9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed5ea6a9fca86f43df161a100e913c29fa9b40c9\">ed5ea6a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform declared agent work through first-party autonomous model/tool execution loops and return operational outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task uncertainty, model responses, tool/retrieval observations, intermediate reasoning, SubAgent results and stopping decisions needed to satisfy the AgentRun input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-local reasoning steps, permitted tool calls or routing actions, interpret observations and decide when an operational answer is complete. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor executed through the first-party Korus Eino pattern runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compiled artifact, prompt/model/tool/knowledge bindings, tool allowlists, iteration limits, worker runtime, gateway, AgentRun lifecycle and trace/artifact persistence. - Closure path: AgentRun reaches the Korus worker → first-party pattern runner invokes the model → model selects a permitted action/tool or final response → tool/SubAgent/model result feeds back into the first-party loop → the loop continues or returns the operational result → AgentRun records the outcome. - Boundary reachability: EinoADKRunner and its built-in pattern handlers are the shipped production worker path at the pinned revision; the release/readiness history explicitly notes that later main replaced the old placeholder with the real Eino runtime and records a passed ReAct smoke test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Korus supplies the loop, context, permitted action surface and feedback transport; the model actor owns the substantive task-local action choice. External model-provider implementation is an environmental dependency rather than a separate agent harness that owns the organizational loop. - Evidence: internal/worker/einorunner.go, internal/worker/reactrunner.go, docs/phase2/agent-patterns-and-a2a-todo.md, docs/releases/v0.1.0-readiness.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic workflow mode and direct single-model fallback are additional supported modes; the A finding is grounded in the shipped autonomous ReAct/router/reflection/plan-execute/tool-calling paths, not in deterministic graph sequencing alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Korus can route work to SubAgents, execute DAG dependencies and pass parent state into nested AgentRuns, but no inspected standard path identifies a concrete interaction-generated conflict, inconsistency or oscillation among distinct S1 units and attenuates that disturbance through an S2-specific relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: router classification, SubAgent references/invocation, workflow graph edges, state propagation and compiler cycle checks. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a router chooses which specialist receives work and a workflow sequences nodes, but delegation/routing/sequencing are not S2 unless tied to a reconstructed inter-S1 disturbance and returned coordination feedback. - Evidence: internal/worker/routerrunner.go, internal/worker/workflowrunner.go, docs/phase2/agent-patterns-and-a2a-todo.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: full Agent Mesh/A2A is explicitly future work at this revision; downstream compositions may introduce S2-specific regulation without changing this repository-relative result.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous or constructor whole-system current-control function is established at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the operator tracks readiness and current run state and enforces configured cancellation, deadlines, retry limits, workspace/provider constraints and lifecycle phase, but these are deterministic reconciliations of already-authored rules/requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: discretionary choices such as desired lifecycle phase, cancellation and retry/deadline parameters remain external spec/operator decisions; the controllers enforce them. - Decision owner: no first-party autonomous S3 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent and AgentRun status, Kubernetes reconcilers, workspace defaults, compiler validation, cancellation, timeout, retry/backoff and runtime status transitions. - Closure path: external specification/current request → deterministic controller reconciliation/enforcement → Agent/AgentRun state changes. No autonomous whole-system current view → discretionary commitment/resource/intervention decision → returned operational change loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: controller authority to retry, fail, cancel or publish according to authored fields is enforcement authority, not evidence that an autonomous actor owns the organizational current-control choice. - Evidence: internal/controller/agentcontroller.go, internal/controller/agentruncontroller.go, api/v1alpha1/agenttypes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the Web Console and operator give humans substantial operational control, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not award S3 parent notation from generic configuration/lifecycle intervention without an evidenced whole-system parent current-control loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently exercise a target Agent revision against a declared evaluation dataset, optionally compare a separate baseline, derive evaluator/threshold results and expose a gate verdict/report as complementary evidence about operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty that ordinary successful AgentRun reporting accurately implies the target revision satisfies externally declared quality/behaviour expectations across representative samples or against a baseline. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the first-party AgentEvaluation contract exposes an audit-specific GatePassed result derived from evaluator/threshold outcomes and baseline comparison; downstream composition must still decide how that verdict changes release/current operation. - Decision owner: no autonomous audit authority is bundled for the final corrective decision. The first-party evaluation controller owns execution and deterministic derivation of the declared audit contract, while the developer/operator must compose the actor/authority and return closure that consumes the verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentEvaluation CRD, Dataset samples/expected values, evaluator and threshold specs, optional baseline Agent/revision, controller-owned evaluation AgentRuns, metric aggregation, report construction and GatePassed status. - Closure path: AgentEvaluation selects target revision + independent dataset/evaluator contract → evaluation controller creates separate managed AgentRuns → results are evaluated/thresholded and optionally compared with a baseline → GatePassed and report are returned on AgentEvaluation status → constructor gap: no standard release/current-control actor consumes that verdict to alter subsequent operation at this revision. - Boundary reachability: AgentEvaluation is a shipped first-party CRD/controller in the standard operator, not repository-only benchmark/test code. The Phase 3 audit confirms evaluation foundations exist while separately identifying the missing release-gate decision/waiver/approval closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Korus intentionally exposes the complementary audit path itself, which is enough for C; it does not ship an autonomous actor that owns the decisive audit-to-operation response, so the path is not A. - Evidence: api/v1alpha1/supportingresourcetypes.go, internal/controller/agentevaluationcontroller.go, docs/phase3/phase3-completeness-audit.md, internal/controller/agentcontroller.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the audit contract can be human-authored and much evaluator logic is deterministic. C is based on the first-party S3-specific feedback construction path, not on treating deterministic threshold enforcement as autonomous organizational judgment. A future release-gate/current-control consumer could change the ownership classification on reassessment. - Claim being audited: the selected current Agent revision satisfies the declared dataset/evaluator/threshold contract and, when configured, compares acceptably with the selected baseline. - Ordinary reporting path: ordinary AgentRuns expose run phase, output, trace references, artifacts and agent revision through the normal execution lifecycle. - Complementary access path: the AgentEvaluation controller independently creates its own labelled managed AgentRuns over Dataset samples and evaluates those outputs against expected values/evaluators/thresholds, with optional separate baseline runs. - Independence boundary: evaluation has a distinct CRD/controller, sample set, evaluator/threshold contract and managed-run ownership from ordinary invocation/reporting. It exercises the same target Agent/runtime intentionally, but does not rely solely on the ordinary AgentRun&#39;s claim of success. - Who acts on findings: no standard first-party release/current-control actor at the reviewed revision; the developer/operator must compose a consumer of GatePassed/reporting, which is the reason for C rather than A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established in the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: reflection can improve the output of the current task and evaluation can identify regressions/threshold failures, but neither path autonomously converts future/environment distinctions into persistent capability changes for later operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor owns a prospective adaptation choice over the organization&#39;s persistent capability/repertoire. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reflection generate/critique/revise loop, evaluation baseline comparison, Agent/Skill/Prompt/Policy declarative resources, revisioned compilation and roadmap/product-development feedback. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding; no outside/future distinction → adaptation option → returned persistent capability change loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reflection is task-local S1 self-correction and evaluation produces audit evidence. Updating prompts, agents, skills, policies or releases from that evidence remains an external developer/operator action at this revision. - Evidence: internal/worker/reflectionrunner.go, internal/controller/agentevaluationcontroller.go, docs/phase3/phase3-completeness-audit.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future release/revision workflows may connect evaluation evidence to adaptation, but design intent and developer iteration are not credited as runtime S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AgentPolicy, workspace provider policy, guardrails, budgets, allowed models/tools and desired lifecycle phase constrain what the system may do, but they are authored externally and deterministically resolved/enforced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: identity, ultimate purpose and top-level policy contents remain with the user/operator/developer outside the first-party autonomous runtime. - Decision owner: no first-party autonomous or operationally closed parent S5 authority established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPolicy CRD, workspace policy/defaults, Agent desired phase, prompt/spec resources, compiler validation and runtime/tool constraints. - Closure path: external authority authors policy/specification → first-party compiler/controller/runtime enforce it. No identity/ultimate-policy issue is autonomously adjudicated and returned through a first-party S5 closure loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static policy objects and hard enforcement express governance constraints but do not themselves own the ultimate policy decision right. - Evidence: api/v1alpha1/supportingresourcetypes.go, api/v1alpha1/agenttypes.go, internal/controller/agentcontroller.go. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human/operator authorship is real governance, but generic configuration authority does not establish Methodology P without a reconstructed identity/ultimate-policy issue and return-to-operation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cf0d144ae67e55ca5541320f7119a0ccf6a7926f/assessments/korus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cf0d144ae67e55ca5541320f7119a0ccf6a7926f\">cf0d144 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T22:58:51Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codejunkie99/agentic-stack\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agentic-stack</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codejunkie99/agentic-stack/tree/8fd5726aaebbd421b835ad1c71f2c66064b74caf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8fd5726aaebbd421b835ad1c71f2c66064b74caf\">8fd5726 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended project/task work using the installed portable context, skills and protocols, observe task/environment results, and return an operational result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task ambiguity, project/repository state, tool/model feedback, remembered conventions, skill selection, failures and other task-local distinctions that require non-preenumerated action choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the concrete reasoning/action response to the current task within the supplied context and constraints and revise that response from model/tool/project feedback. - Decision owner: the autonomous model-backed agent actor reached through the shipped standalone Python/conductor mode; external compatible harness agents can occupy the same operational role but are not needed to establish the first-party standalone path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context-budget builder; portable memory/skills/protocol files; configured provider selection; execution logging; permissions/hooks; optional recall/search; and adapter/install machinery. - Closure path: standalone-python/run.py receives a task → conductor.run builds the first-party system context → llm.callmodel invokes the configured model actor → the actor makes the task-level response decision → conductor logs success/failure and returns the result to the operating session. - Boundary reachability: the repository ships adapters/standalone-python/run.py, .agent/harness/conductor.py, and .agent/harness/llm.py as a supported adapter path. It therefore reaches an autonomous model actor without requiring the organizational internals of Claude Code/Codex/other adjacent harnesses. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while leaving conductor, context assembly, local files and logging intact leaves no mechanism that makes the open-ended task decision. The deterministic harness supports and records S1; the agent owns the operative discretion. - Evidence: README.md; adapters/standalone-python/run.py; .agent/harness/conductor.py; .agent/harness/llm.py; docs/architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the provider service and model implementation are external infrastructure. This finding credits the shipped first-party adapter/control path that places the model actor in the operational role; it does not inherit provider-side organizational functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate workspace/branch interference among distinct mutating bounded-loop S1 runs by assigning each run an owned isolated Git worktree and retaining that isolation across retries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent or overlapping maker runs against one project could collide in the active checkout, overwrite one another&#39;s working changes, or make changed-path verification ambiguous. - Distinct S1 units: separate bounded loop runs, each with its own maker agent process and task-local operational work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-workspace mutation would couple otherwise independent runs through the same checkout/files; the design explicitly states that separate runs never share a worktree and action-capable loops require isolation because reliable per-run change attribution matters. - Attenuating coordination relation: createworktree() allocates a unique run-id-specific branch/worktree from the validated repository base; the runner executes that run&#39;s maker and verifier in the owned path; other runs receive different paths. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the coordination result is not merely recorded metadata: the owned worktree becomes executionroot, so all later maker retries, verifier checks and changed-path policy for that run occur inside the isolated workspace rather than the shared active checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path is specifically designed to prevent cross-run mutation interference and preserve independent run identity/forensics. It is not credited from generic delegation, a queue, or the shared memory layer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and bind a run to a unique owned worktree/branch so mutating S1 cells do not operate on the same checkout. - Decision owner: no autonomous coordination owner is shipped for this right. The first-party loop runtime deterministically applies the isolation contract; the operator/developer supplies the maker profiles and initiates the runs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run-id generation; repository/common-dir identity verification; safe worktree root checks; unique branch naming; checkpointed ownership metadata; changed-path collection; refusal-safe cleanup. - Closure path: a mutating loop is started → runner creates/verifies a unique worktree → that path becomes the maker&#39;s execution root → retries/verifier/checker remain bound to the same run worktree → independent runs receive separate worktrees, attenuating shared-checkout interference in subsequent S1 action. - Boundary reachability: the bounded-loop commands, worktree implementation, default L2/L3 worktree contract and checkpoint machinery are shipped first-party and are installed by loop init; a user supplies compatible maker commands but does not have to invent the isolation/feedback path itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing any autonomous coordinating agent leaves materially the same unique-worktree decision and enforcement. Conversely, the shipped default loop role profiles are placeholders rather than a first-party autonomous coordination actor. The function-specific S2 path is therefore constructor-level C, not A. - Evidence: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-agentic-loops-meta-harness-design.md; harnessmanager/loops/runner.py; harnessmanager/loops/worktrees.py; .agent/loops/harnesses.json. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this S2 witness is specifically the bounded-loop multi-run mode, not the mere fact that many adapter types can share .agent/. The current release does not supply an autonomous agent that decides cross-run coordination policy, so the state is intentionally limited to C.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control loop is established across the full assessed agentic-stack operational population. - Disturbance / variety regulated: individual loop budgets, attempts, policy violations, cancellation and approval are regulated; local dashboards summarize activity across harnesses; neither surface closes whole-system resource/commitment/prioritization control over all current S1 cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor inside the credited boundary is shown deciding current priorities, resource allocation, commitments, synergy or intervention for the whole heterogeneous set of agent sessions represented by the installed stack. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the declared system boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-loop attempt/runtime/token/output breakers; per-run cancellation; project-wide bounded-loop pause-all; loop status; local data-layer dashboards and Mission Control read projections; operator approvals. - Closure path: no whole-system S3-specific decision-and-return path spans the complete assessed operational population. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: pause-all is a strong operator intervention but it only governs agentic-stack bounded-loop runners, while Mission Control/data-layer visibility also represents external adapter sessions and cron work that the pause path does not control. Per-loop breakers enforce preselected constraints rather than own whole-system managerial discretion. - Evidence: docs/data-layer.md; harnessmanager/loops/commands.py; harnessmanager/loops/runner.py; harnessmanager/missioncontrolserver.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a narrower recursion containing only bounded loop runs could expose operator stop/status control, but this assessment fixes the shipped installed agentic-stack project boundary rather than silently shrinking the system to make a positive S3 witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge a maker&#39;s claim that a bounded software task is complete after deterministic verification, then return approve/reject/escalate findings into retry or completion control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, semantically inadequate changes that nevertheless pass deterministic tests, repeated flawed approaches, and cases requiring human judgment. - Claim being audited: the maker&#39;s current attempt satisfies the original task and constraints after the deterministic verifier has passed. - Ordinary reporting path: maker process result, changed-file list, deterministic verifier stdout/stderr/exit status and checkpoint/event history. - Complementary access path: a separately named checker profile is launched in a fresh process after verifier success with review instructions and access to the same isolated project state; its structured verdict is parsed separately from the maker result. - Independence boundary: the first-party protocol requires checker profile name separation and a fresh process, but permits the same underlying binary and ships only placeholder custom-agent profiles. Sufficiently independent autonomous reviewer identity/access must therefore be composed by the user/operator. - Who acts on findings: runner.py deterministically maps APPROVE to completion, REJECT to feedback and another bounded maker attempt, and ESCALATE/malformed output to a paused run requiring human judgment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: maker self-confirmation and semantic defects not captured by deterministic verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue the complementary semantic audit verdict that determines whether the attempt completes, retries with feedback, or escalates. - Decision owner: constructor-level only in the standard distribution. A checker agent can own the judgment once a genuinely independent compatible checker profile is supplied; the bundled placeholder does not itself establish that autonomous owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic verifier gate; distinct checker profile key; fresh process invocation; structured verdict grammar; attempt budget; checkpoint/event log; worktree preservation for inspection. - Closure path: maker attempt → deterministic verifier pass → checker process reviews task/project outcome → APPROVE completes, REJECT becomes feedback in the next maker prompt, or ESCALATE pauses for human judgment → subsequent operation changes according to the finding. - Boundary reachability: checker invocation, result parsing, retry/escalation closure and the checker role contract are all shipped in the bounded-loop runtime. The missing piece is the independently controlled checker actor/profile, which is intentionally user-composed through the shipped profile schema. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific audit/feedback path exists, but the default checker command is a custom-agent placeholder and same-binary checker use is explicitly permitted. Agentic-stack therefore cannot claim that the standard distribution itself owns an independent autonomous audit judgment. - Evidence: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-agentic-loops-meta-harness-design.md; harnessmanager/loops/runner.py; .agent/loops/harnesses.json; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic verification alone is not credited as S3. The C state specifically reflects the first-party checker/audit decision path while preserving the unresolved independence/actor composition boundary.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party externally/prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established that models future environment distinctions, develops options and returns them into current organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the system does learn from past execution, stage recurring patterns, recall memory, flag repeated failures and permit skill rewrites, but those are retrospective/internal learning paths rather than a demonstrated outside-and-then intelligence loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is shown owning a prospective environment/adaptation judgment satisfying the S4 witness at this boundary. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: episodic/semantic memory; staging-only dream cycle; Skillforge/self-rewrite hooks; failure counters; data flywheel exports; dashboards/KPIs; upgrade and skill-manifest machinery. - Closure path: no S4-specific external/future distinction → option generation → return-to-current-capability loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous agents may use memories or create/update skills during task work, but the reviewed standard distribution does not show a distinct prospective environmental intelligence function whose options are fed back to alter present organizational capability. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; .agent/memory/autodream.py; .agent/skills/skillforge/SKILL.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the repository contains future-facing design/spec material and an optional data flywheel, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat roadmap/design documents, retrospective learning, or future training possibilities as S4 without the operational prospective loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is established for the installed agentic-stack organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, approval-required actions, deny paths, budgets and user preferences constrain operations, but they are static/operator-authored policies or operational approvals rather than S5 identity deliberation and closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or parent path is shown receiving an identity/ultimate-policy issue, deciding it as legitimate ultimate authority, and returning that decision to govern subsequent operation as an S5 loop. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: human-owned permissions.md; pre-tool-call enforcement; loop constraints/budgets; approval gates; personal preferences; installer configuration; user-controlled feature toggles. - Closure path: no qualifying S5 decision-and-return path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permissions.md explicitly says humans edit it and the agent may not modify/bypass it. That makes ultimate constraints externally configured, but static parent authorship and ordinary action approval do not by themselves establish parent-governed S5 under Methodology 0.3.6. - Evidence: .agent/protocols/permissions.md; docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-agentic-loops-meta-harness-design.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user clearly remains an ultimate authority outside the autonomous task loop and can edit policies, approve sensitive actions or stop work. Those ordinary operator powers are not published as S5=P without evidence of a function-specific identity/ultimate-policy matter and closed return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20b70cdc8b17b6a3c9568bf24a1d5eef2507e6d1/assessments/agentic-stack.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20b70cdc8b17b6a3c9568bf24a1d5eef2507e6d1\">20b70cd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:48:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Meta-Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/tree/0cbc31e97c9e6d24232d1dc754827c02e1ec415c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0cbc31e97c9e6d24232d1dc754827c02e1ec415c\">0cbc31e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. The system&#39;s purpose is automated search over task-specific harnesses. Its supplied loop invokes a proposer agent, validates generated candidates, benchmarks them and iterates against the current frontier without requiring a human decision each iteration. That closed search trajectory is the operational transformation at this boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Candidate systems, proposer calls and benchmark jobs are stages or alternatives inside one search operation. The reviewed framework does not establish two or more operational S1 units plus a concrete mutual-interference regulation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. Frontier bookkeeping, iteration control and candidate validation govern the search procedure itself. They do not establish a distinct metasystem actor with whole-organization current view and authority over shared organizational resources, commitments or priorities.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Validation benchmarking is the normal production feedback used by the search. A held-out test path exists, but it is explicitly run only when the evolution run is finalized and frozen against further evolution; its findings therefore do not feed back into subsequent current control within that run. No separate corrective audit closure is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. The historical positive classified evolutionary harness optimization as S4. Under the function-first criterion, candidate generation and validation against task benchmarks are the primary operation of this meta-harness itself, and the information source is task-internal performance evidence. No separate outside-and-then environmental intelligence function relating future/external change back to present organizational capability was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Domain specifications, objectives, benchmark configuration and user initiation define the search problem, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529/assessments/meta-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529\">911ebc9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T00:26:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ScaleML/AgentSPEX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentSPEX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-01\">2026-10-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ScaleML/AgentSPEX/tree/db99b7b6802fe15ef2997c82ec1d49e5228128f6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db99b7b6802fe15ef2997c82ec1d49e5228128f6\">db99b7b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a user task/workflow objective into an answer or tool-mediated environmental result through model-driven reasoning/planning/action with returned execution feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, workspace/environment state, tool results/errors, model outputs, variable/context state, plan failures and bounded execution conditions encountered while completing one objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive model outputs, next task actions/tool calls, and in agentic-loop mode the decomposition/plan content used to pursue the current objective. - Decision owner: the configured autonomous LLM actor invoked by AgentSPEX&#39;s first-party execution/agentic-loop paths. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSPEX, LLM executor, workflow interpreter, tool registry/MCP integration, agentic-loop exploration/decomposition/planning, task board, plan verifier/executor, checkpoints, traces, limits and sandbox/runtime support. - Closure path: user goal/current context → first-party runtime calls the model → model selects output/tool action or task plan → runtime validates/executes permitted work → tool/environment/step results return into workflow/agent context → model revises/continues or synthesizes the final result. - Boundary reachability: the documented src/harness/run.py entry directly selects normal modes or loop mode and invokes the shipped runtime. No downstream orchestration package is needed beyond a configured model/provider and external tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining the deterministic interpreter, plan validator and tool executor removes the substantive task/decomposition/next-action judgment; those host mechanisms enforce or apply decisions rather than substitute the same judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/harness/run.py; src/harness/agent.py; src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference and external tools may be outside the repository, but the first-party distribution concretely closes their decisions/results inside the supported task execution loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 disturbance-attenuation function is established at the assessed task recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete interaction-generated conflict/oscillation between distinct credited sibling S1 units is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML parallel, gather, foreach, sub-workflows, task-board ordering, variable dependencies, isolated sandboxes and concurrency controls can schedule/combine work but do not establish S2 by themselves. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generated subtasks and branches are decomposition of one objective. The reviewed runtime does not identify peer-S1 interference and feed an attenuation decision back into those peer operations. - Evidence: docs/workflow-language.md; src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py; src/harness/agenticloop/taskboard.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream workflows can compose multiple agents, but generic composition/fan-out is not credited without a concrete S2 disturbance-and-attenuation witness owned by AgentSPEX.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control function is established above the single task/workflow organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current subtask state, step dependencies, retries, plan validity, token/tool limits, checkpoints and optional plan approval are regulated locally within one objective rather than through a higher whole-organization control loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct whole-system judgment over current resources, commitments, priorities, constraints or cross-S1 intervention is established at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task board, generated plan, deterministic interpreter/control flow, plan verifier, configuration limits, checkpoint/resume, user plan approval and execution lifecycle events. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Whole-system current view: TaskBoard exposes the current decomposition/status/results for one user objective, but that is the operating agent&#39;s own task state rather than a distinct metasystem view over multiple autonomous operational commitments. - Current-control decision scope: current plan/subtask sequencing and bounded execution of one task; no separate current resource/commitment authority at a higher recursion is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM decomposition/planning is part of S1 task pursuit. Deterministic status tracking, workflow scheduling and a human approval gate do not establish a separate S3 function or parent S3 mode. - Evidence: src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py; src/harness/agenticloop/taskboard.py; src/harness/agenticloop/planexecutor.py; src/harness/run.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the word “plan” or task-board visibility is not treated as S3 when it is internal decomposition/control of one S1 objective.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit loop is established at the standard runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: structural workflow defects and ordinary runtime errors are checked/recorded, but no independent path is shown auditing actual operating claims and returning complementary findings into later organizational control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying independent audit judgment-and-return right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agentic-loop PlanVerifier, standalone Lean AgentVerifier, checkpoints/traces/replay, dashboard/structured events, benchmark evaluators and demo-specific reviewers/refiners. - Closure path: not applicable; no standard runtime path closes ordinary operational evidence → complementary independent access → audit finding → returned corrective/control decision. - Claim being audited: not established as a runtime organizational claim distinct from ordinary execution validity. - Ordinary reporting path: model/workflow outputs, tool results, task-board/status events and execution traces. - Complementary access path: no qualifying standard operational path established. PlanVerifier and Lean verification inspect plan/workflow structure rather than independently observing actual S1 behavior. - Independence boundary: not established for a runtime audit loop. Benchmark/demo evaluators may have separate criteria but are adjacent application/evaluation systems rather than a generic standard runtime feedback owner. - Who acts on findings: no qualifying complementary findings path is established; structural validation blocks malformed plans before execution and ordinary runtime errors remain on the normal control path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: structural verification and telemetry can support correctness/observability but do not establish the S3 function without an independent observation/claim path and return closure. - Evidence: src/harness/agenticloop/verifier.py; verifier/README.md; src/harness/run.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the standalone Lean verifier can prove structural graph properties and a downstream benchmark can separately grade an AgentSPEX workflow, but neither is credited as a standard complementary operational feedback loop at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established that turns prior environmental evidence into a persistent changed AgentSPEX capability or control strategy for later operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task state, stored episodic information, checkpoints, trace data and within-task planning can influence continued work, but no distinct future-facing capability adaptation is generated and durably applied. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is established selecting a persistent future runtime/harness change from accumulated external evidence. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: episodic memory store/search/retrieve, checkpoints, resume, trace replay, workspace state, LLM plan revision and reusable YAML/submodule definitions. - Closure path: not applicable; no observed external evidence → prospective adaptation option → durable capability/strategy mutation → later operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory and recovery preserve information/current work; planner revisions pursue the current objective. They do not modify the future harness or generate a persistent adaptation option from environmental evidence. - Evidence: src/sandboxvm/tools/memory/episodic.py; src/harness/run.py; src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: persistent episodic data can improve later context if a workflow chooses to retrieve it, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not equate durable memory/context reuse with S4 capability adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed first-party runtime loop is established for deciding or reaffirming AgentSPEX&#39;s ultimate identity, purpose or foundational policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: YAML/system prompts, tool availability, model/configuration, runtime limits, sandbox constraints and optional user plan approval shape operation, but they are externally authored inputs/gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor receives an identity/ultimate-policy issue and returns an authoritative foundational decision governing subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow configuration, system prompts, tool/MCP registration, environment variables, sandbox/VM restrictions, plan approval and structural workflow validation. - Closure path: not applicable; no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the LLM can plan/decompose work within a supplied objective and workflow/tool boundary, but no supported path lets it own the mandate itself. Human approval of one plan is a bounded action gate rather than a runtime S5 identity-policy loop. - Evidence: README.md; src/harness/run.py; docs/workflow-language.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong static rules/configuration and human gating can enforce policy but do not establish ownership of ultimate policy/identity under the Profile closure test.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6b3d8c0916f09f4f067aca760a92db3904ec93e/assessments/agentspex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6b3d8c0916f09f4f067aca760a92db3904ec93e\">d6b3d8c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T14:58:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/raroque/boop-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Boop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/raroque/boop-agent/tree/31979130b1371acd9defbea115279a06c63c1fb4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 31979130b1371acd9defbea115279a06c63c1fb4\">3197913 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Interpret a user&#39;s message or a previously specified automation task and produce a context-sensitive answer, research/tool-mediated result, or staged external action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Diverse message intents, incomplete task context, integration returns and failures, action confirmation and changing tool observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The runtime-invoked autonomous dispatcher decides answer versus delegated work and writes the task it delegates; each execution agent chooses how to respond and which exposed tools to invoke after seeing returns. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed dispatcher/execution agents in the first-party Boop turn path. The external inference service executes cognition, but Boop actually assembles and invokes these actors; deterministic spawn, tool filtering and storage are support/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded history, model/runtime adapter, permissions/allowed-tool lists, Convex messages/logs, scoped integrations, timeouts/abort, draft persistence, cron and usage accounting. - Closure path: Incoming user message → handleUserMessage → model chooses response or spawnagent → spawnExecutionAgent returns tool/result/status → dispatcher returns user-facing reply; independently, a due configured automation starts the same execution agent and stores/notifies outcome. - Boundary reachability: Both handleUserMessage and spawnExecutionAgent are imported by shipped server routes/scheduler. The dispatcher has an actual model-runtime call and first-party spawnagent callback, not merely a README proposal. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model-backed actor leaves webhook delivery, a database, static schedules and tools but no discretionary interpretation of arbitrary user goals or next tool selection. The developer and user set the operating envelope; the model-backed actor owns ordinary in-envelope task choices. - Evidence: model-selected dispatcher tools and result; tool-bearing worker and return; runtime provider selection. - Basis: Structural first-party assembly of a model-to-tool-to-result loop; explicit packaged operating mode. - Confidence: High for code-path existence; no claim of empirical agent effectiveness or verified vendor integration. - Caveats: This is an application orchestration of installed Claude/Codex runtime agents, not first-party inference weights or ownership of the external SDK&#39;s independent internal architecture. The shipped Boop dispatcher/worker integration is the credited loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately established first-party regulation of interference/oscillation among distinct operational work cells at the Boop service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent delegated tasks might conflict over external accounts, memory or repeated notifications; such possibility alone is not an observed S2 witness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No source-shipped conflict judgment/negotiation or returned inter-S1 adjustment distinguished from parent-to-child delegation, tool scope and message deduplication. - Decision owner: Not established. A single dispatcher assigns tasks; user approval applies to external drafts, not general inter-worker coordination. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-agent Convex logs/status, tool-scope filtering, Sendblue/webhook dedup, scheduled runs, draft status, shared memory and worker abort. - Closure path: Generic execution results return to the dispatcher; the reviewed code does not feed a specific inter-S1 conflict resolution into the next actions of two independently operating work cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model task delegation and static duplication gates do not become S2 because two work cells can exist or a message can be sent twice. - Evidence: dispatcher spawn; independent scheduled agent runs; proactive email delivery dedup. - Basis: Bounded negative, source-first functional search. - Confidence: Medium-high for the reviewed standard runtime; no assertion about downstream private integrations. - Caveats: External backends may impose locks/quotas and operators can manually coordinate tasks; neither establishes a Boop-owned inter-S1 conflict closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current organization-wide discretionary control of resources, priorities or commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale jobs, worker failures, usage growth and concurrent tasks can threaten the active service, but packaged responses are local lifecycle enforcement or human-selected settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Whole-system reallocation or intervention judgment is not supplied. Abort/retry applies to an individual worker; stale-heartbeat failure marking is deterministic. - Decision owner: User/operator for runtime configuration and manual task intervention; code for fixed stale-state and scheduling rules. No autonomous or first-party complete S3 parent-control loop is shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent state dashboard, token/usage summaries, abort controllers, configured model, tool whitelist, heartbeat sweep and retry endpoint. - Closure path: A failed/stale work cell can be marked failed, retried or cancelled locally, but the observed path does not choose/revise current whole-system commitments based on a current global view. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A dashboard plus per-agent lifecycle API is observation/enforcement, not a managerial decision right over the whole; the dispatcher selects work for a user request, not S3 across active operations. - Evidence: stale-heartbeat sweep; worker cancellation/retry; server endpoints; dashboard metrics. - Basis: Bounded negative under first-party standard modes. - Confidence: Medium; operator intervention rights exist but are not shown as whole-current S3 governance. - Caveats: Operator self-hosting or dashboard administration is not by itself an evidenced S3 P mode at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: The reviewed service has quality/checking and memory consolidation, but no sufficiently independent complementary audit of ordinary S1 claims feeding a separate current-control path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect agent outputs and erroneous stored memories could distort future replies; memory maintenance checks a proposed rewrite, not a separately sourced audit of overall agent operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A memory-proposer/adversary/judge sequence does make a model judgment over merge/prune proposals; it is not presented as an independent audit of S1 reports/actual external results at the assessed service recursion. - Decision owner: The model-backed judge for the narrow memory-maintenance operation; no evidenced complementary system-level S3 audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Memory snapshots, proposer/adversary prompts, JSON verdict parse, Convex memory mutations, agent logs and ordinary tool traces. - Closure path: Judge-approved memory edits return to memory state, not as audit findings to a distinct current-control owner that can correct S1&#39;s operational assertions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple roles and an adversarial prompt do not prove an independent complementary organizational channel; all stages work on the same memory-proposal payload and shared original memory list. - Evidence: proposer/adversary/judge and memory apply; ordinary agent logging. - Basis: Structural negative at declared recursion; a narrower memory-maintenance criticism path does exist. - Confidence: Medium, specifically limited by the chosen service-level organizational boundary. - Caveats: The consolidation judge is not ignored; its evidenced judgment is credited to memory maintenance, not automatically S3 complementary access to external operational reality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party outside/future sensing → adaptation-option judgment → update of Boop&#39;s present operational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing provider capabilities, external user needs and integration landscapes might demand future adaptation, but the shipped routines react to current events/tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The proactive classifier judges whether a current email merits notifying the user; memory consolidation revises stored context; neither owns an externally prospective capability-adaptation choice. - Decision owner: Operator configures provider/integrations and upgrades the app; ordinary model work cells reason about present user tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gmail rubric, preference memory, cron, local memory decay/consolidation, runtime/model selection UI and installed integration catalog. - Closure path: Current emails may trigger messages; memory edits affect later prompt context, but no prospective environmental model develops an adaptation option and returns it to operational capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Proactive alerts are external event reactions; storing corrected memories is not a future-oriented capability redesign; runtime switching is user configuration. - Evidence: proactive classifier; memory consolidation; runtime settings. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can task an execution agent to research the future, but an arbitrary S1 research output is not an installed harness S4 adaptation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate-identity/policy dispute and legitimate authority return path is established for the personal-agent service. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Sending messages or accessing sensitive accounts requires limits, but these are ordinary action permissions and configured tool safety rather than a demonstrated S5 deliberation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user confirms a draft&#39;s ordinary external action; the dispatcher is restricted to spawn/memory/automation tools. No service-level identity change or ultimate-policy deliberation is routed and returned. - Decision owner: User/operator controls service purpose, credentials, settings and action confirmation externally. The model-backed dispatcher owns bounded S1 task choices, not ultimate identity rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Draft staging, senddraft/reject flow, allowed/disallowed tools, browser setting and isolated Codex read-only mode. - Closure path: Confirmation leads to a worker executing a pending draft; settings alter runtime options. Neither is identity/ultimate-policy question → legitimate parent decision → binding return to future operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A prompt role, static tool restriction, local runtime switch or user saying &quot;send it&quot; does not amount to S5 even when security-relevant. - Evidence: draft confirmation and commit; dispatcher allow/deny list; Codex isolation/config. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operator governance and software-project maintainer policy remain real but are not part of an evidenced operational S5 parent return loop of the assessed service.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1d27f0bee31bcf3379c314e30cc8e91b212b7e02/assessments/bo/boop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1d27f0bee31bcf3379c314e30cc8e91b212b7e02\">1d27f0b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T18:46:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/threatclaw/threatclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ThreatClaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/threatclaw/threatclaw/tree/71540e5da1fc4dd5ff62c37842de25efab67a3f1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 71540e5da1fc4dd5ff62c37842de25efab67a3f1\">71540e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous bounded model-based incident investigation and evidence/tool selection with live feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown incidents, evolving signals, noisy security telemetry, ambiguity about CVE/alert severity, missing evidence and failing skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured first-party model-driven investigation decides whether more context is needed and names investigation skills/parameters; loop reinvokes model with executed skill results and returns a verdict. Distinct scheduled ReAct cycle also chooses model-driven threat correlation/actions. - Decision owner: ThreatClaw-owned agent invocation and its configured model reasoning, with bounded prompts and tool dispatch; not an externally authored vendor agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dossier, preflight enrichment, skill registry, timeouts, max iterations/max calls, typed response parser, security mode and safe-action whitelist. - Closure path: asset dossier → model suspicion/skill request → first-party bounded skill execution → returned observation in following prompt → revised verdict/action proposal → recorded incident. - Boundary reachability: Booted Intelligence ticker can launch runinvestigation from selected current asset, with semaphore and incident store; runreactcycle also has an installed ticker route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides actual investigative steps based on in-run observations rather than consuming only fixed rule tables; default preflight is deterministic and does not remove model discretion on subsequent skills. - Evidence: src/agent/investigation.rs; src/agent/reactrunner.rs; src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/agent/investigationskills.rs. - Basis: Explicit reachable source-level model/skill/feedback loop. - Confidence: High for the implemented configured model path; no live external inference or detection quality measured. - Caveats: Operational mode and backend availability matter; Analyst mode disables ReAct, and malicious output is constrained by a separate guard.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent harmful concurrency and oscillation between distinct current incident/skill/model operations competing for one finite inference/worker resource or repeated asset claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: CPU-served Ollama cannot safely process unbounded concurrent investigations; simultaneous tasks could overload model service or duplicate work on a single incident/asset. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party semaphore, atomic claim, queue capacity and cooldown rules determine when other in-flight S1 investigations may proceed; model does not choose conflict mitigation discretion. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party worker-pool/Intelligence admission and suppression paths under operator-provided limits. - Boundary reachability: Standard service starts the Intelligence ticker and installed task pools; Intelligence spawns incident investigations under the global semaphore, the active registry checks for already investigated assets, and queue supervisors atomically claim and execute work. Their effects gate actual future agent investigation, not an unreachable utility module. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: INVESTIGATIONSEMAPHORE with one permit across investigation requests, WorkerPool permits across task-kind workloads, atomic claimnexttask, retry-on-fail, asset dedup registry and versioned cooldown. - Closure path: competing S1 submissions/active investigation status → permit or dedup/queue decision → task blocks/skips or is admitted → completion frees permit and updates task/asset state → subsequent S1 attempt changes. - Distinct S1 units: Separately launched dossier investigation tasks and graph/LLM/skill workloads; each can execute model/tool-bearing incident operations. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous calls to scarce one-at-a-time CPU LLM and repeated evaluations of the same incident degrade reliability, create feedback storms and compete for active current security monitoring. - Attenuating coordination relation: One-permit serialized model investigations, atomic claim/worker capacity, per-asset cooling and load-based admission. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Semaphores release on completion; verdict/error cooldowns are written back and gate future investigations; queued tasks enter running/done/failed states. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: These paths regulate concrete concurrency collisions and duplicate incident investigation, beyond data transport or merely sequencing a single authored workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A statically configured bounded structure enforces noninterference; it is S2=C, not an agent self-negotiating arbitration. - Evidence: src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/agent/taskqueue/pool.rs; src/agent/taskqueue/supervisor.rs; src/agent/taskqueue/backpressure.rs. - Basis: Structural default operating concurrency and source-evidenced failure mode; even if a particular incident pair has not been reproduced in this review. - Confidence: High for named shared LLM semaphore and atomic worker claims. - Caveats: Does not prove distributed multi-replica safety or all races are eliminated; the asset dedup cache is in-memory.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Current whole-installation prioritization, admission and escalation of monitored asset investigations from a global security situation and finite investigation capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Many simultaneous findings/alerts, competing asset risks, finite investigation throughput, alert fatigue and high-severity attack needing faster monitoring. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party scoring/threshold scheduler reads the combined current asset picture, changes polling interval, selects the highest-risk monitored asset, starts/defers incident work, suppresses predictive-only/duplicate/unowned targets, and can propose HITL response. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party Intelligence Engine plus configured rules/operator mode. The incident model supplies conclusions but does not own whole-installation resource allocation. - Boundary reachability: The production startbackgroundservices launcher starts spawnintelligenceticker; the ticker awaits runintelligencecycle and uses the resulting global SecuritySituation to choose a next interval, highest-priority asset, inventory/dedup eligibility, incident creation and real runinvestigation before the next cycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SecuritySituation aggregation, decidenotificationlevel, sorted/maximum risk asset selection, asset inventory gate, dedup/active registry, dynamic interval control, spawned investigation and remediation guard. - Closure path: whole-installation findings/alerts/graph view → asset score and alert/priority selection → actual incident investigation/deferral with capacity guard → stored verdict/result → updated next-cycle current situation and scheduling. - Whole-system current view: Aggregated findings/alerts grouped by canonical monitored asset, global score, attack flags and current investigation registry across the single SOC installation. - Current-control decision scope: Present monitoring cadence, which asset is eligible for action now, when incident investigation is admitted, how duplicate/noise work is suppressed, and when the SOC operator receives an escalation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decisive selection is expressed in first-party rules (maxby, thresholds, cooldown, inventory membership), not dynamically chosen by an S3 policy model. The resident model controls a specific S1 dossier, not the overall current commitment selection; therefore S3=C. - Evidence: src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/channels/web/handlers/threatclawapi.rs; src/agent/modemanager.rs; src/agent/remediationengine.rs. - Basis: Installed reachable whole-SOC scoring/admission/intervention path. - Confidence: Medium-high for bounded current priority regulator; not asserted as full staffed-SOC governance. - Caveats: No first-party strategic budget bargaining or durable organizational project-level S3 autonomy. In default Investigator mode meaningful disruptive write remediation remains human authorized.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Challenge the model investigator&#39;s incident claims with independent structured dossier/citation/telemetry evidence and feed a binding corrective verdict in enabled Strict mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model-fabricated CVEs, MITRE references or citations; confident unsupported &#39;confirmed&#39; verdicts; erroneous false-positive dismissal despite Sigma/KEV/ML corroboration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic reconcileverdictfull rules compare the model snapshot with score/Sigma/CISA KEV/graph, citation checks, actual skill-call counts, typed validation; Strict overrides the conclusion when justified. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic reconciliation and separately computed corroborating signals, not another model auditor. Operator chooses Strict, Lenient or Off configuration. - Boundary reachability: The actual incident runinvestigation path parses the model answer, builds validators/citation report from stored dossier and evidence, invokes verdictreconciler::reconcileverdictfull and, whenever Strict outcome.apply is true, overwrites parsed verdict, severity and confidence before final incident state is emitted. This is not only an isolated unit test. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed validators, citation tracker, SignalsSnapshot, rule  adjustments and investigation result assembly. - Closure path: model-issued verdict → alternative dossier/graph/ML/Sigma/citation/skill evidence and validation → reconciler chooses challenge → under Strict, revised severity/verdict/confidence overwrites parsed → final incident classification/escalation; discrepancies logged. - Claim being audited: Whether the LLM&#39;s reported incident verdict and supporting evidence are credible enough to drive severity/escalation. - Ordinary reporting path: Model-issued parsed.verdict, severity, confidence and narrative from iterative S1 investigation. - Complementary access path: First-party independent deterministic alert/risk context, KEV presence, graph context, citation pointers verified against real dossier, actual executed-skill counter and typed structural checks. - Independence boundary: Materially different evidence and rules compared to LLM self-judgment; not a separate human/reviewer deployment. The evidence is provided by the same installation and may be incomplete; rules skip missing graph context and do not establish independent source provenance for every field. - Who acts on findings: runinvestigation applies outcome.log.reconciled to its live parsed verdict only if outcome.apply (Strict); that final verdict feeds incident outcome. Lenient only logs and Off disables. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Rules bind correction from complementary data but no independent LLM agent chooses it, so S3=C, not S3=A. - Evidence: src/agent/investigation.rs; src/agent/verdictreconciler.rs; src/agent/evidencetracker.rs; src/agent/validators/mod.rs; src/agent/validationmode.rs. - Basis: Explicit source-attested alternative-evidence challenge in actual investigation path, binding only under Strict mode. - Confidence: High for existence of Strict-mode override; limited/conditional as audit coverage. - Caveats: Default validation mode must not be assumed strict without checking runtime config; this is a supported conditional C path, not a mandatory protected independent auditor. An internal source bug could contaminate both the model dossier and verifier input.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supported first-party strategic/prospective organizational adaptation decision with a two-way return to S3 established at this revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing new IoCs, Sigma/MITRE/KEV feeds, future threats and feature/vendor rule updates are externally relevant, but ingestion alone does not prove S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The optional updater follows an external supplier&#39;s signed pack manifest/version and installs verified bytes; it does not evaluate future alternatives, select a capability-investment/adaptation option or negotiate it with current control. Operational thresholds are prewritten rules. - Decision owner: External supplier for package contents/version, administrator for support key/enablement, deterministic first-party signature/hash/version synchronizer for installation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: 6-hour updater schedule, signed-manifest verification, per-pack registry, anti-rollback and verified install, enrichment periodic resync. - Closure path: External feed → validated data/rule pack → current detection configuration may change, but no first-party evidenced prospective option synthesis/selection → S3 dialogue → organizational adaptation decision/feedback cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic update is a supply synchronization mechanism, not an autonomous organization-level S4 regulator merely because it ingests evolving threats. - Evidence: src/agent/ruleupdater.rs; src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/agent/sigmaengine.rs; src/agent/modemanager.rs. - Basis: Bounded source review of strongest apparent candidate, signed pack updater, plus enrichment and mission/planning alternatives. - Confidence: Medium; would change with explicit documented first-party adaptive option/test/apply path. - Caveats: Operational detection improvement is possible through new signatures; the negative state is about missing S4 organizational function, not inability to update rules.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party decisive ultimate identity/ethos/policy-setting and returned governance loop shown for this deployed SOC installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model safety, operator action privileges, immutable-soul tampering and tool permission boundaries are regulated by static configuration and enforced action checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Project-supplied AGENTSOUL.toml is hash-verified, while the operator sets autonomy mode/approval. Neither a live first-party final-policy conflict adjudicator nor binding identity-policy adaptation/return is shown. - Decision owner: External project developer and site SOC administrator configure fixed identity and operational constraints; no dedicated S5 in assessed first-party runtime proven. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Compile-time soul hash, immutablerules prompt injection, mode settings, whitelist, nonce/atomic approvals, protected infrastructure guard. - Closure path: Static rule check or human consent gates a current command, but no first-party ultimate issue → responsible higher-level governing decision → identity/policy settlement → returned organizational response loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static &#39;soul&#39; data, boot hash and human approval do not themselves create Beer S5; S1 can propose actions but cannot redefine ultimate system identity. - Evidence: src/agent/soul.rs; AGENTSOUL.toml; src/agent/modemanager.rs; src/agent/remediationengine.rs; src/agent/remediationguard.rs. - Basis: Bounded negative analysis of first-party identity, security and approval surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high for reviewed operating scope. - Caveats: A customer&#39;s actual security policy and supplier stewardship are separate organizations; don&#39;t inherit them into the installed software automatically.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cca7c7d47ae0afc940a1f68ff3bea6a71287cef8/assessments/threatclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cca7c7d47ae0afc940a1f68ff3bea6a71287cef8\">cca7c7d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T16:31:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/harnessclaw/harnessclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">harnessclaw/harnessclaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/harnessclaw/harnessclaw/tree/44d01c1fa2193d1d4f479327cc15cf1bec09851c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 44d01c1fa2193d1d4f479327cc15cf1bec09851c\">44d01c1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform model-selected tool and delegation actions to address user tasks within a supported desktop agent session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user task instructions, tool/browser/file outputs and provider or subagent responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the native engine&#39;s LLM loop selects tool calls and continuation; desktop user gates particular actions. - Decision owner: model-driven Harnessclaw Engine agent within the first-party distributed desktop/runtime composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawned native engine, WebSocket session/card transport, permission requests, tool-result return, persisted session forest and provider configuration. - Closure path: user/session message → engine model/tool cycle → tool or delegated result → engine continuation/finish → streamed cards and session result into desktop UI. - Boundary reachability: release workflow bundles a real platform engine binary, Electron resolves and spawns it, and client starts authenticated sessions; engine tag v0.0.22 provides first-party source evidence for the observed distribution path. Frontend shell alone is not the claimed agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: engine model/tool selection is discretionary, unlike static WebSocket card parsing or Electron launch code. - Evidence: packaged binary resolution and process, process spawn, engine query-loop README. - Basis: structural supported packaged-mode wiring and a named observed engine release; not a live provider execution. - Confidence: medium for stable source-to-binary correspondence given frontend&#39;s non-pinned runtime selector; high that a supported installed agentic mode is intentionally shipped. - Caveats: do not generalize to unknown future engine assets, or treat mock/demo/event translation as a substitute for actual agent execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party S2-specific inter-S1 disturbance attenuation and returned adjustment is evidenced in the bounded available runtime path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: subagent scheduling, card visibility and ordered task dispatch are generic work management until a concrete cross-worker collision/relation is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agent may dispatch subagents; deterministic scheduler selects sync/async execution strategy, but does not demonstrate an inter-unit conflict resolution decision. - Decision owner: native parent agent for delegation, scheduler for dispatch, not a distinct S2 attenuation actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent cards, event streams, scheduler Dispatch/Subscribe, tool permissions. - Closure path: subagent tool requests and results return to the parent agent; no evidenced collision signal changes a second worker&#39;s subsequent operating conduct. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple workers and ordering are not, by themselves, viable inter-S1 coordination. - Evidence: client subagent event handling, engine scheduler contract. - Basis: reviewed supported frontend and tagged runtime source. - Confidence: medium; current release selection is not immutable in this frontend ref. - Caveats: a future coordinator with concrete inter-S1 collision control would need a new evidence-specific assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no proven whole-system current resource/commitment control loop beyond user configuration, individual approvals and delegated task management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, session failures, step retry/replan budgets, runtime restart and task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user can approve a local tool or answer a plan/step prompt; engine controls per-turn limits and task dispatch. - Decision owner: user for local approvals, native model for operational task decisions, static engine for dispatch/limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permissions, step-decision WebSocket bookkeeping, session reconnect, scheduler tool. - Closure path: UI replies unblock a pending individual tool/step; no demonstrated view/regulation of shared current whole-system commitments with a legitimate parent S3 decision loop. Tagged runtime&#39;s plan-coordinator tool mode returns unavailable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an &#39;agent team&#39; UI and a human gate are insufficient without a whole-system current-control decision owner and returned resource intervention. - Evidence: step/plan UI response, runtime explicit unavailable plan mode. - Basis: structural, release-bounded. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: re-evaluate after engine coordinator restoration and explicit version pin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently owned complementary operational check of agent results is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emitted tool/card summaries, developer diagnostics and prompt validation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: same runtime emits progress; user may inspect outcomes; no distinct audit owner with alternative access channel. - Decision owner: none in the installed task operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event log, card forest, error reports and tests. - Closure path: ordinary execution telemetry is displayed but independent findings are not returned into a corrective management decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: event viewing and user approvals lack a separate auditor and independent evidentiary path. - Evidence: stream/card conversion, session logs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for observed runtime. - Caveats: manual inspection by user is not credited as independent S3 agent authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective environment-scanning and capability-adaptation loop beyond model task planning or maintainer releases. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new skills/models/providers, application updates and future tools. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/maintainers install skills, configure model settings and ship releases. - Decision owner: adjacent human user/developer, not a first-party future-intelligence actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skills market, engine config, updater, release pipeline. - Closure path: configuration/release can change future sessions; no autonomous outside-and-then option-development decision visibly returns to current operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning an assigned coding task is S1; installing a skill is a user action, not system-level S4. - Evidence: skills market, runtime and update code. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a later engine release may add such a loop; the front-end selector is not pinned.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate-purpose or organizational identity decision loop at one desktop agent-session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission modes, tool allow/deny, user questions and agent personas. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user sets tool permissions and responds to individual approvals; engine applies rules. - Decision owner: task user and deterministic enforcement, not a designated S5 ultimate-authority mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool allow/deny, session user prompts, profile/skill settings. - Closure path: decisions unlock specific tool or conversation operations; no identity-policy dispute returns to govern S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission is operational risk control, not identity-level policy/constitutional legitimacy. - Evidence: permission client, permissions at engine tag. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: software community governance is outside this one-session system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2edb9e43f23b0390ac188bdf9730d996e3075dc2/assessments/ha/harnessclaw-harnessclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2edb9e43f23b0390ac188bdf9730d996e3075dc2\">2edb9e4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T16:36:30+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Rustam-Z/hamroh\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hamroh</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Rustam-Z/hamroh/tree/0970d3a3dc8ff50970998515afe4ef79510f397e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0970d3a3dc8ff50970998515afe4ef79510f397e\">0970d3a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform conversational, retrieval, memory and scheduled assistant work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing requests, external observations, chat context, tool failures and incomplete long tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tools, interpret their results, send/withhold an answer and continue long work through heartbeat. - Decision owner: configured model-backed Claude agent running as the deployment&#39;s S1 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Engine buffering, worker stream transport, local MCP tools, SQLite/memory, tool allowlists and failure recovery. - Closure path: inbound messages/reminders reach worker.send; agent tool results and further messages drive subsequent decisions; structured turn results return to Engine and user-facing tools deliver outcomes. - Boundary reachability: startup builds the shipped worker/tool/prompt configuration and CcWorker.start launches that operational actor. This credits the assembled Hamroh mode, not uninspected Claude governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hamroh&#39;s transport alone cannot choose task-dependent tool calls or useful replies; those choices are made by its configured agent. - Evidence: hamroh/startup.py; hamroh/ccworker/spec.py; hamroh/ccworker/worker.py; hamroh/engine/engine.py; prompts/system.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Claude Code/model service is a required external implementation dependency; no live provider execution or inherited higher-function claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no shipped function-specific inter-S1 interference regulation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one shared conversation engine serializes ordinary turns; optional subagents perform delegated tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no specific cross-unit conflict-resolution right is packaged. - Decision owner: parent agent chooses subtask scope; developer selects queue/tool restrictions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: single-turn buffer, reminder row claims, shared tool surface and read-only-by-default subagent guidance. - Closure path: child results return as task data; reminder callbacks regulate delivery lifecycle, not distinct operational units&#39; mutual adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: message sequencing, duplicate-reminder prevention and generic delegation safety are not an S2-specific organizational path. - Evidence: hamroh/engine/engine.py; hamroh/scheduler/reminderscheduler.py; prompts/subagents.md; plugins.json.example. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The shared-identity destructive-write warning is real exposure, but no actual inter-unit conflict/oscillation and corrective coordination relation is reconstructed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: owner-governed current regulation of the whole installed assistant&#39;s admission and shared execution continuity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: long/stuck shared turns, growing inbound queue, degraded delivery and continuing commitments across authorized chats. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret global operational state and choose pause/resume of inbound operations or replacement of the shared execution session. - Decision owner: authenticated deployment owner identified by HAMROHOWNERID. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: health readout, owner-ID command guard, dispatcher paused gate, worker reset/restore and status replies. - Whole-system current view: health combines current engine duration/queue with deployment-wide last send, reflection reminder status and rate-limit notices; audit/log views add failures and storage changes. - Current-control decision scope: all Telegram inbound admission for this bot and the common Claude session; not per-tool approval and not a claim that pause also stops the reminder scheduler. - Closure path: owner observes current readout and selects a command; pause/resume changes dispatcher.onmessage handling, and resetsession restarts the shared worker with restore context. Subsequent operations use that returned state. - Boundary reachability: registered owner commands are direct runtime handlers available in the documented self-hosted mode, without composing a new supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the owner exercises current intervention discretion; deterministic worker recovery, health transport and command enforcement do not own that judgment. - Evidence: hamroh/telegramio/commands.py; hamroh/telegramio/dispatcher.py; hamroh/engine/engine.py; hamroh/ccworker/worker.py; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Parent mode is a supported human control loop, not evidence of autonomous S3, a recorded intervention trace, or every kill/permission command being S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit judgment with returned corrective control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the same assistant reviews prior outputs and owners can request diagnostic readouts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reflection judgment belongs to the producing assistant; /audit formats failures/backups/footprint without a separate audit mandate/judgment loop. - Decision owner: same operational agent for self-review; owner for subsequent supervision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: raw message/reaction database, /audit and /logs, backups and reflection reasoning records. - Closure path: self-review proposals enter owner adaptation/policy decisions; deterministic diagnostics expose data but do not install independent audit ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-reflection or a command named audit is not sufficiently independent complementary audit. - Evidence: skills/self-reflection/SKILL.md; hamroh/telegramio/commands.py; hamroh/tools/databasequery.py; prompts/system.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Optional child isolation is task-context isolation; it does not by itself supply an independent auditor or different operational access.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: owner-governed prospective adaptation of assistant behavior/procedures to its user environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections, negative reactions, language/tone mismatches and rules that would misfire in future situations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve, reject, refine or redirect the proposed capability/behavior change after scenario testing. - Decision owner: deployment owner; agent owns investigation, scenario generation and proposal, not final adoption. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: protected reminder seed, skill loader, SQL/memory access, reflection log, skill/instruction write tools and restart/reload. - External distinction: actual Telegram user corrections/reactions and observed mismatches with user/group expectations, rather than internal memory growth alone. - Future / prospective distinction: each candidate is tested on 10–20 on-target, adjacent, off-target and boundary scenarios to anticipate later misapplication. - Adaptation option generated: proposed refined standing rule, reusable skill/playbook or environment-specific memory update with an explicit proposed destination. - Path back into current capability / S3: owner reply selects the option/target; agent writes the approved skill or rule; current tools read memory/skills and owner restarts the worker to load modified overlay/index. - Closure path: reflection steps 4–6 route options to the owner, parse the returned decision and execute only approved changes; step 7 states activation/restart and rollback. Subsequent work uses the changed procedure/instructions. - Boundary reachability: startup auto-seeds the packaged skill; scheduler injects a real reminder; plugins.toolgroups.subagents=true exposes the Agent tool required by the heavy-skill prompt. This is a supported first-party flag, not custom authority wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without owner adoption the proposals remain pending; agent scenario reasoning and storage machinery do not own the adaptation decision. - Evidence: skills/self-reflection/SKILL.md; prompts/system.md; hamroh/startup.py; hamroh/scheduler/reminderscheduler.py; hamroh/tools/skills.py; hamroh/tools/instructions.py; hamroh/ccworker/spec.py; docs/tools.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S4=P is for the supported subagents-enabled reflection mode. Default example disables Agent despite prompt-required background spawning; no successful default-mode reflection run is asserted. Write authorization is prompt-enforced and restart is human-owned.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: parent-governed deployment identity and durable operating-policy closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposed changes to bot persona, language/ethos and session-wide standing behavior, including policy proposals produced by reflection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the legitimate owner chooses which standing identity/behavior rule governs this bot and may dictate wording or reject it. - Decision owner: deployment owner for ultimate policy within the parent-supplied immutable base constraints; no agent-owned ultimate authority is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner metadata instructions, project overlay read/append/rewrite, backups, size/path restrictions and fresh-spawn prompt composition. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether to change the bot&#39;s identity/personality or a durable behavior default for every session, not whether one ordinary tool call may proceed. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: HAMROHOWNERID holder governs the installed bot&#39;s project overlay; base system.md and host-level enablement remain higher/developer constraints. - Return-to-operation path: owner request or approved/refined reflection proposal → first-party instruction tools update project.md → owner restart/new spawn → composesystemprompt concatenates the returned identity/policy overlay for subsequent operation. - Closure path: documented owner persona edits and rule adoption are persisted with backups; the worker loads that decision on the next spawn, rather than merely displaying a policy file. - Boundary reachability: the shipped project example explicitly holds Identity &amp; personality; system.md exposes the owner-only editing process and startup wires InstructionsStore into the local tool surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent applies the owner&#39;s standing decision; it cannot unilaterally promote the rule or alter immutable base policy. - Evidence: prompts/project.md.example; prompts/system.md; skills/self-reflection/SKILL.md; hamroh/tools/instructions.py; hamroh/storage/instructionsstore.py; hamroh/startup.py; hamroh/ccworker/spec.py; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: P is bounded to the installed bot&#39;s identity/standing policy. Owner-only instruction writes rely on model policy, not a cryptographic/code authorization check; this is not a security guarantee or blanket S5 credit for access settings.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f819c48d0734987250ad11729a8cb6a7b73db509/assessments/ha/hamroh.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f819c48d0734987250ad11729a8cb6a7b73db509\">f819c48 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T21:57:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/venkatapgummadi/agentflow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentFlow (venkatapgummadi)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/venkatapgummadi/agentflow/tree/81c07248082130f6528e335b24be0f1d85c8a1f6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81c07248082130f6528e335b24be0f1d85c8a1f6\">81c0724 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose a task-specific API operation plan for the user&#39;s intent and deliver it into actionable enterprise integration steps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: diverse natural-language requests and API domains require different operations, dependencies, parameters and capability tags. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the semantic operations, ordered dependencies and parameters from user intent; these choices materially affect which requests are planned and executed. - Decision owner: the configured model in supported LLMIntentParser or LLM-first HybridIntentParser mode. The integrating developer chooses the model provider and permitted connector set; the deterministic planner maps model output to endpoint candidates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: JSON schema prompt, normalization, parse confidence/fallback, API discovery, capability matching, plan DAG, bounded parallel execution, connector invocation, circuit-breaker/retry and result journal. - Closure path: user intent → model-generated operation payload → createplan DAG → executor&#39;s connector calls → step results/validation returned to the caller; the payload changes the current action path. - Boundary reachability: the shipped orchestrator directly calls parseasync for injected LLM/hybrid parsers and passes operations to the shipped planner/executor. A documented CallableLLMProvider is the supported provider boundary; an out-of-repository model service supplies inference but no separately orchestrating host agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: replacing the model with the default parser yields only heuristic decomposition; a real selected model owns the discretionary semantic operation set in the explicitly wired mode. The later deterministic router/executor and the human&#39;s choice of connectors do not own that model choice. - Evidence: orchestrator, LLM parser, provider adapter, documented deployment, executor. - Basis: structural first-party model invocation and documented configuration. - Confidence: medium; source-path reachability is strong, external model effectiveness not tested. - Caveats: default operation is deterministic and DeterministicMockProvider is not evidence of agent autonomy. Inference is a one-shot planning pass, not a model/tool observation/replanning loop; some executor branches remain placeholders.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no established inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation among distinct credited autonomous operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent DAG calls can contend for external resources or share data dependencies; merely launching them in parallel does not establish S2. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitrate specific inter-unit interference and revise later operating relations. - Decision owner: no distinct S2 owner; developer-defined plan dependency and semaphore enforce scheduling. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: planner DAG dependencies, executor ready-step selection and concurrency semaphore, shared context and event journal. - Closure path: ready steps are scheduled/serialized, but no S2-specific disturbance detection → discretionary coordination response → changed later S1 behaviour is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planner/executor/validator are stages of one workflow, not independently autonomous sibling S1 businesses; neither a DAG edge nor a semaphore inherits a coordinating decision right. - Evidence: planner, executor, context. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: high for the reviewed standard run. - Caveats: an embedding application can assemble distinct agents and a richer S2, but that composition is not in this assessed library distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no established whole-system current-operations regulation across multiple credited S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: API failures, per-step resource consumption, endpoint health, concurrency and task completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: use a whole-system current view to revise resources, priorities, commitments or constraints for distinct operational S1 units. - Decision owner: host developer configures limits/connectors; deterministic executor/router enforces predefined per-run responses. No first-party S3 discretionary owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxparallelsteps, budget router, dynamic endpoint scores, retry/circuit breaker, event journal and per-run result envelope. - Closure path: routine execution can retry or mark failures, but no owner evaluates a portfolio-level current view and returns an authoritative new multi-S1 control arrangement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the code&#39;s AgentOrchestrator name and budget/latency calculations do not transfer the choice of shared commitments to an autonomous actor. - Evidence: orchestrator, budget router, dynamic router. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: these are useful S1 task execution controls. A business host may add supervising governance but it is not a credited first-party parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary, sufficiently independent evidence challenge to ordinary operational reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed or misleading API results, false completion and missing output fields. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect operational reality and feed findings back into control outside the routine producing/reporting path. - Decision owner: normal ValidatorAgent executes fixed rules in the same run; no independent audit owner is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: result/event journal, completion/failure/data-presence validation and user-supplied rule callbacks. - Closure path: ordinary validator returns a validation report with the result, but its path is the standard pipeline and is not a separate complementary audit with independent access and control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the validator an agent does not create independent evidence or autonomous audit judgment. - Evidence: validator, orchestrator, context. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a separate operator audit using journal and raw API state could be built, but it is outside the shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no established external-and-prospective adaptation option loop returning new capability to current operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing API latency, failure rates, costs, available endpoints and future integration demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: turn external/prospective distinctions into capability alternatives, select an adaptation, and feed it back to current S3/operations. - Decision owner: developer chooses connector/model/weight strategy; score updates and adaptive weight optimization are deterministic local response algorithms, not an agent-owned future capability decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: router metric history and AdaptiveWeightOptimizer, API discovery, fallback and connector configuration. - Closure path: scores can alter endpoint preference in an embedding context, but no external future model, alternatives evaluation and S4-to-current-capability decision/return are supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: online score adjustment is current routing optimization; an LLM interpreting one user&#39;s present task is not strategic external sensing. - Evidence: adaptive optimizer, dynamic router, LLM parser. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the repo describes adaptive routing; no live SLA/tradeoff quality result was measured here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party operationally closed identity/ultimate-policy authority at the selected orchestration-run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: enterprise data-access boundaries, developer policies, model use and high-stakes API action choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue and return a decision governing subsequent operation. - Decision owner: implementing enterprise/developer chooses connectors, budgets and compliance mode outside the library; no supported in-run ultimate-policy actor is present. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider prompt/schema, deterministic validation, parser fallback, connector authentication, fixed budgets, repository policy/docs. - Closure path: static configuration constrains API execution but no first-party parent identity-policy question → legitimate authority → returned organization-wide change path is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task parsing and route choice are operational discretion, not legitimate constitution/identity setting; configuring a policy externally does not establish S5=P without closed runtime governance. - Evidence: orchestrator, hybrid parser, LLM provider boundary, security policy. - Basis: bounded negative including parent-mode inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user&#39;s enterprise may have actual higher-recursion S5, but no corresponding first-party path was observed here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c10bad4fe6e60dd9c0984a009920b62045f842f1/assessments/ag/agentflow-venkatapgummadi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c10bad4fe6e60dd9c0984a009920b62045f842f1\">c10bad4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T18:32:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lloydzhou/bash-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bash-agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lloydzhou/bash-agent/tree/e0062d42f7016f40639a8e69070131d521194cc1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e0062d42f7016f40639a8e69070131d521194cc1\">e0062d4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Repeatedly choose and execute developer-task tools based on model outputs and changing code/tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coding task, repository mutations, host shell result, model response and operator constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a function-specific decision from observed changes to currently operating conditions. - Decision owner: First-party Bash agentloop model actor with llmcall and direct tool dispatch; the OpenAI-compatible provider supplies inference. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agentloopstream, tooldispatch, storeconvaddtoolresults, agentloop parent process, bounded MAXTURNS and tool permissions. - Closure path: User objective → model completion/action → tool execution → persisted tool result/history → another model call and task-specific next action or finish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped Bash agentloopstream repeatedly sends persisted conversation state to the model, executes tool calls via tooldispatch, records results, and continues to a new model turn; child subprocesses run that same loop. - Boundary reachability: The declared first-party packaged CLI or runtime directly exposes and runs the described mode without borrowing any external agent task loop. - Evidence: src/agent.sh; docs/BASHARCHITECTURE.md; README.en.md. - Basis: source-first structural evidence of pinned standard-distribution modes. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Pinned first-party Bash distribution only, not external agents or separately implemented ports&#39; unverified behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuate actual interference between independently adapting child coding S1 workers and return revised work scopes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple child edits may overlap within the same host workspace, while asynchronous reports arrive at different times. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a function-specific decision from observed changes to currently operating conditions. - Decision owner: The parent model chooses initial child tasks and receives FIFO results; deterministic session-depth guard and background process signaling are not inter-S1 conflict-resolution owners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgent fork/subsession, NOTIFYFIFO and AGENTRESULT delivery, subagent depth cap, parent async result injection. - Closure path: Parent launches independent child goals → children run their own model/tool loops → notifications return summaries to parent; no specific inter-child collision sensor/adaptive corrective-return path is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SubAgent creates multiple independent Bash child model loops in background sessions; they share host filesystem and results arrive asynchronously, but no write-conflict sensing and model-owned inter-child rescope/repair is established. - Evidence: src/agent.sh; docs/sessions.md; README.en.md. - Basis: source-first structural evidence of pinned standard-distribution modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Pinned first-party Bash distribution only, not external agents or separately implemented ports&#39; unverified behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Control whole-current active child S1 commitments and resources with discretionary team-wide intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coding task, repository mutations, host shell result, model response and operator constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a function-specific decision from observed changes to currently operating conditions. - Decision owner: No distinct first-party owner with the full function-specific decision authority is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in bounded agent loop, tool dispatcher, session history and deterministic operational gates. - Closure path: A bounded tool/execution path exists without the required separate organizational judgment and corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Although a parent can launch children, the shipped Bash loop lacks a whole-child-roster management interface that selects and returns real current priority/reallocation decisions across still-active agents. - Evidence: src/agent.sh; docs/sessions.md; docs/BASHARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: source-first structural evidence of pinned standard-distribution modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Pinned first-party Bash distribution only, not external agents or separately implemented ports&#39; unverified behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently inspect operational evidence from a complementary vantage and return an audit-based correction to the present S3 controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coding task, repository mutations, host shell result, model response and operator constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a function-specific decision from observed changes to currently operating conditions. - Decision owner: No distinct first-party owner with the full function-specific decision authority is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in bounded agent loop, tool dispatcher, session history and deterministic operational gates. - Closure path: A bounded tool/execution path exists without the required separate organizational judgment and corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime stores model/tool history and shows tool output, but no separately owned audit observer with direct evidence inspection and a returned S3 corrective decision is shipped. - Evidence: src/agent.sh; docs/BASHARCHITECTURE.md; README.en.md. - Basis: source-first structural evidence of pinned standard-distribution modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Pinned first-party Bash distribution only, not external agents or separately implemented ports&#39; unverified behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Sense future external environment and autonomously revise Bash agent&#39;s future capabilities or organizational strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coding task, repository mutations, host shell result, model response and operator constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a function-specific decision from observed changes to currently operating conditions. - Decision owner: No distinct first-party owner with the full function-specific decision authority is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in bounded agent loop, tool dispatcher, session history and deterministic operational gates. - Closure path: A bounded tool/execution path exists without the required separate organizational judgment and corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context compaction, forked sessions and on-demand skills preserve knowledge and current task continuity, but do not select or promote future-facing strategic change. - Evidence: src/agent.sh; docs/compact-analysis.md; docs/sessions.md. - Basis: source-first structural evidence of pinned standard-distribution modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Pinned first-party Bash distribution only, not external agents or separately implemented ports&#39; unverified behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve ultimate legitimate purpose and policy disputes on behalf of the whole agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coding task, repository mutations, host shell result, model response and operator constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a function-specific decision from observed changes to currently operating conditions. - Decision owner: No distinct first-party owner with the full function-specific decision authority is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in bounded agent loop, tool dispatcher, session history and deterministic operational gates. - Closure path: A bounded tool/execution path exists without the required separate organizational judgment and corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Allowed tools and operator-supplied credentials/approval gates bound execution rather than deciding or revising overarching identity/mission authority. - Evidence: src/agent.sh; docs/bash-tool-policy.md; README.en.md. - Basis: source-first structural evidence of pinned standard-distribution modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Pinned first-party Bash distribution only, not external agents or separately implemented ports&#39; unverified behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/290afbd78c088620d55c505b6098fb4d480b9ebb/assessments/bash-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 290afbd78c088620d55c505b6098fb4d480b9ebb\">290afbd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-21T17:07:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cordfuse/politik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Politik</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-21\">2026-10-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/tree/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247\">c1fa731 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform semantic task work inside a governed session using a model-enabled, stateless role worker. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unstructured draft/governance/task requirements, tool results and git work needing substantive model interpretation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Worker chooses content and permissible git/tool actions for the task; human retains higher-level assent/constitutional rights. - Decision owner: The model-backed worker launched by the first-party Politik runner and scoped to a session, role, standing orders and constituency. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Known CLI registry, election/lock, session state/Charter gate, prompt composer, subprocess adapter, Hansard/LEDGER records. - Closure path: runTurn → spawnAgent → result/files committed or inspected → Hansard/state and subsequent prompts consume retained results/record; point-of-order can suspend further work. - Boundary reachability: src/cli.ts command politik run invokes runTurn which invokes the real subprocess adapter using supported Claude Code/Codex/etc command definitions. It does not require an unpublished first-party autonomous service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The task-semantic model worker is executed in a first-party session path, not merely described in a prompt example; process remains external inference but operated within shipped session orchestration. - Evidence: https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/cli.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/runner.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/agents.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/spawn.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/README.md - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level runnable path, not a live AI experiment or proof of physical-world effect; full OS sandbox containment is not claimed..\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced continuing inter-S1 operational-unit interference regulated by reciprocal feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Contention for constituency seats and disagreement in a Division, not automatically conflict between continuing viable operational departments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A candidate S2 should resolve a concrete inter-S1 disturbance and return adjustment to future independent operations. - Decision owner: Election/slot lock, protocol vote tally and Speaker handle governance work, not proven S2 discretionary owner across continuing agentic S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: nodeLockFs, constituency election, quorum/Division, Hansard, nested session registry. - Closure path: Slot election admits or defers one stateless worker turn; vote tally and Assent decide a motion. Neither closes an interference-feedback relation between multiple continuing S1 operations. - Boundary reachability: No positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Session concurrency/seat contention and voting are real mechanisms but do not constitute a demonstrated S2-specific regulation across two viable agentic operating units at this declared boundary. - Evidence: https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/runner.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/election.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/division.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/federation.ts - Basis: structural. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: A future service with continuously operating teams and concrete reciprocal collision handling would require a new pinned assessment..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No ongoing whole-system management of present operational resources, commitments or interventions across independent continuing S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent cost/time bounds, task execution and governance vote disagreements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Whole-of-organization current control would decide resource/accountability interventions and return them into S1 operation. - Decision owner: Runtime enforces Charter limits; Speaker enacts motions but these are not shown as continuous whole-fleet S3 budget/priority bargaining across operating departments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Endurance limits, cost LEDGER, deterministic sessions, quorum, suspension and actor constraints. - Closure path: Runtime may deny/stop one session, and a human may assent, but the reviewed reference has no instantiated whole-organization present performance-control loop. - Boundary reachability: No positive S3 or parent S3 mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static per-session cost ceilings and authorized motions cannot be promoted to ongoing S3 of a multi-S1 organization without present whole-system decision evidence. - Evidence: https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/runner.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/charter.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/ledger.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/cli.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/POLITIK-ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: Real governance/approval mechanisms exist; this negative is specific to their lack of the required whole-current S3 regulation at the declared recursion..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of an S3 whole-system managerial account is operationally returned to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coerced exits, strategic withdrawals, manipulated governance records or false agent reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect reality by an alternative channel, reach independent judgment and feed findings to S3 correction. - Decision owner: Pure reviewIntegrity detects patterns in the same Hansard and surfaces reports; Speaker judgment about what to do is separate and not shown as a complementary audit-into-S3 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Append-only-intended Hansard/git commit history, integrity checks, immutable ledger, containment-after-execution detection. - Closure path: Integrity findings can be printed/surfaced, but source does not establish a distinct independent alternative access path, manager accountability and corrective S3 operation from them. - Boundary reachability: No positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Verification against the ordinary governance record, even valuable bad-faith pattern detection, is not independent complementary S3 audit at the session recursion. - Evidence: https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/integrity.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/runner.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/ledger.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/escalation.ts - Basis: structural. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: The record includes meaningful attribution and fraud signals; these are not negated by withholding S3..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped prospective external-environment intelligence loop selecting organizational adaptation options and returning them into present capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential changes in protocol context and future risks beyond the current motion/task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Identify environmental change and prospective options, then engage present S3 for accepted capability change. - Decision owner: Per-session agent may research a commissioned task, while protocol choice and Charter terms are configured by humans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reference protocols, optional SCM projection, session registry, research/game-theoretic conceptual notes. - Closure path: Agents can formulate motions/current plans, but no first-party external future monitoring/option-generation/adoption feedback loop is wired into present system capability. - Boundary reachability: No positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current political deliberation and research design are not by themselves S4 of the harness; game theory experiments explicitly remain future research. - Evidence: https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/README.md; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/RESEARCH.md; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/runner.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/protocol.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/cli.ts - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: An agent may write prospective content as an S1 task outcome; that is not an organizational adaptation capability..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Human constitutional Speaker establishes binding session identity/standing orders and closes escalated identity/policy questions at the declared session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Attempts to revise legitimacy of actions, Charter authority, session suspension or conflict beyond agent/role mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Speaker/legitimate human authority determines constitutional rules or ruling on higher-authority matters, and the accepted decision returns to agent execution. - Decision owner: Human AUTHORITY Speaker as legitimate parent of the governed session. Model workers may submit motions/Point of Order but cannot grant their own Assent or bypass suspension. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CHARTER.md validated at turn start, role/constituency checks, Speaker-only Assent/Ruling, STATE CONVENED/SUSPENDED, append-only Hansard and optional SCM projection. - Closure path: Agent files a scope-overrun Point of Order → session SUSPENDED and notified → Speaker issues ruling/decision → state returned to CONVENED → next run re-reads Charter and ruling-rich Hansard/standing orders and is governed accordingly. Separately, carried identity/standing-order changes require human Assent and become authoritative recorded enacted decisions. - Boundary reachability: runTurn rejects suspended sessions, reads CHARTER.md, injects Standing Orders and Hansard into agent prompt; src/escalation.ts implements authorized Speaker Ruling and return; grantAssent and CLI enforce named constitutional authority. The parent path is shipped even without hosted SCM. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decisive ultimate-policy right stays with a human, not the model or deterministic gate. A real return-to-operation path distinguishes this parent S5 from mere prompt/static policy text. - Evidence: https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/charter.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/runner.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/escalation.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/division.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/cli.ts; https://github.com/cordfuse/politik/blob/c1fa7312c75599d72b187bf20088f51f5dda2247/src/state.ts - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: Only legitimate session-level constitutional decisions count. Ordinary per-motion task approvals or votes are not automatically S5; changing arbitrary real-world enterprise constitutions is not claimed..\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/33f8f5ad06309fe1d46f77a5ac0213eaaa9406ac/assessments/po/politik.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 33f8f5ad06309fe1d46f77a5ac0213eaaa9406ac\">33f8f5a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T19:06:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TejasQ/basically-ai-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mini-ai-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TejasQ/basically-ai-harness/tree/ea40e4dec975e6a0c56412bf8217581a1761329c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ea40e4dec975e6a0c56412bf8217581a1761329c\">ea40e4d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete a browser task using model-selected environment actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Page state, redirects, already-voted stories and browser/tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which browser tool and arguments to use next; interpret observations and finish. - Decision owner: The model-backed agent called by runLoop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-bound tool registry, BrowserSession, message history and model transport. - Closure path: toolcalls → execute → tool/error messages → next completion; finishreason stop returns the answer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model, the registry/browser cannot decide task-specific actions; the installed entry wires that choice without user composition. - Evidence: package.json; agent/7-index.ts; agent/5-loop.ts; agent/1-tools.ts; agent/browser.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Static source evidence; no live model/provider run or empirical viability claim. - Boundary reachability: package.json agent script directly executes agent/7-index.ts, which calls the first-party runLoop with first-party tools.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No inter-S1 disturbance-regulation path in the selected single-agent run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential browser task changes are within one agent&#39;s local operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sequential tool execution within one agent is not coordination among distinct operational units. - Evidence: agent/7-index.ts; agent/5-loop.ts; agent/browser.ts; package.json - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system current-control authority beyond the local agent loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Run duration, context growth and errors would need bounded current regulation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model&#39;s next local tool action and stop are operational S1 choices. No supervisor allocates resources or revises commitments on behalf of a larger current system. - Evidence: agent/5-loop.ts; agent/7-index.ts; agent/3-context.ts; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary independent audit of browser-agent reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A model may report success without changing the intended page state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool traces and DOM tools belong to the acting agent&#39;s routine path. The fixed-dataset eval does not audit this run or feed a corrective decision back. - Evidence: agent/5-loop.ts; agent/1-tools.ts; agent/7-index.ts; eval/4-runner.ts; package.json - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No externally prospective adaptation of the harness&#39;s present capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Web pages change, but sensing them is the current task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Navigation and story inspection produce the task outcome, not future-environment options with a capability/control return. - Evidence: agent/1-tools.ts; agent/3-context.ts; agent/5-loop.ts; agent/7-index.ts; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task and model are fixed in the entry; generic instructions bound behaviour. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The context prompt and hard-coded task are configuration, not an authoritative identity-policy issue/decision/return loop. - Evidence: agent/3-context.ts; agent/7-index.ts; agent/2-model.ts; README.md; package.json - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cbf0837361bfd748028485abe821aa1b6f804725/assessments/mi/mini-ai-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cbf0837361bfd748028485abe821aa1b6f804725\">cbf0837 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T21:02:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Caveman Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman-code/tree/3a21be115c32aa27288c8ed6d860d9aa1b65d2c5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3a21be115c32aa27288c8ed6d860d9aa1b65d2c5\">3a21be1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Deliver requested repository investigation and implementation through autonomous model choices and feedback-controlled file/shell actions, including bundled specialist subprocesses. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository contents, coding specifications, tool failures, agent-generated results, changing file state, incomplete plans and session context pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The root model selects tool/action/delegation steps after observations; child models separately choose steps within their assigned read-only or implementer tool permissions. - Decision owner: First-party root AgentSession model loop, with model-driven subagent subprocesses for delegated specialist coding tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read/edit/write/bash/search tools, task and agent delegation, bounded child spawn, provider selection, parent event/session continuation, worktree scope, goal iterations and state persistence. - Closure path: Coding instruction → model action or specialist delegation → local tool/subagent executes → output/status or error returns → model chooses correction or completion; goal mode may initiate another bounded pass. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual next coding and tool decisions depend on model-generated reasoning and observed results, not a fixed shell script alone; policies and recursion limits constrain its actions. - Boundary reachability: The released CLI constructs all tool definitions and loads bundled specialist agent definitions; model tools include task and code/file tool paths, while goal mode invokes the real CLI for new iterations. - Evidence: tool feedback loop, first-party tool registry, bundled agent loader, child dispatch. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for real distributed first-party model/tool loops; no model/provider live outcome was run for this assessment. - Caveats: Child ability depends on selected provider/model, task tool exposure and permissions; task success and reported benchmark results were not reproduced.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuate actual interference between concurrently viable coding S1 cells and return coordination adjustments to the affected cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneous code-edit collisions, incompatible child patches, sibling priorities and shared workspace contention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The root can delegate and inspect results, but no shipped conflict-observation/settlement procedure that actively adjusts sibling coding operations is established; separate worktrees prevent simple simultaneous writes only in configured modes. - Decision owner: No qualifying distinct S2 decision owner. Root parent is a task delegator; worktree helpers and concurrency mapper are mechanical isolation/scheduling. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parallel/chain fan-out, MAXCONCURRENCY=4, bounded recursion, opt-in implementer worktree, no-change auto-cleanup, status and parent-to-background-agent mailbox. - Closure path: Parent delegates → each child acts in an isolated or shared checkout → summarized success/failure and worktree path return to parent. There is no demonstrated interference detection → negotiated correction → multiple S1 resynchronization closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The isolation/transport machinery can reduce risk but does not own adaptive cross-S1 conflict damping; parent model&#39;s general task choices are still S1-level delivery unless an explicit regulation loop is shown. - Evidence: worktree fallback and returned artifacts, parallel OK/FAIL aggregation, worktree cleanup keeps changed branches, bundled worktree implementer. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence; worktrees demonstrably implement isolation, not measured collaborative conflict closure. - Caveats: A human or custom manager can merge worktrees and arbitrate children, but that external operation cannot be credited as installed S2 autonomy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Select binding whole-current commitments, priorities and scarce resources among independent operating work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple delegated work requests, concurrency saturation, task failure, goal nonprogress and token/time budget pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-based delegation is within the user&#39;s current coding request; max task/concurrency/iteration/budget constants enforce caps rather than strategic reallocations of viable units. - Decision owner: Root coding S1 for task choices; deterministic goal-runner, resource counters and dispatch scheduler for fixed operational limits; no distinct S3 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task mode/queue, max seven children/four concurrent, goal ledger, wall-clock and no-progress pause, output statuses, tool permissions. - Closure path: Goal/task call → bounded child or iteration → result ledger/status → same coding task continues, stops or pauses; no autonomous whole-current cross-S1 commitment reallocation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A parent can issue more tasks, but no evidence distinguishes managing the present whole organization from executing and decomposing one coding goal. - Evidence: bounded parallel dispatcher, goal caps and progress ledger. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A deployment that overlays durable task portfolio governance is outside this frozen product boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Audit S3 management&#39;s operating claims from a distinct, complementary evidentiary channel and correct S3 when claims diverge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect patches, missing edge cases, failing tests, misleading status and inaccurate progress assessment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The shipped read-only reviewer and critic report code/plan defects to the parent, not independently challenge S3&#39;s actual allocation or operating reality. - Decision owner: Specialist task reviewers acting within delegated coding S1 work, with no S3 claim/audit/corrective management right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bundled reviewer, critic and tester prompts, read-only tool restrictions, logs, goal progress signal and local tests. - Closure path: Parent requests code review → reviewer reads code/plan → task-level findings return to parent coding model; no current S3 decision → independent alternate operating reality → binding S3 correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent LLM criticism exists, but independence from the author of code does not entail complementary independence from a whole-current management claim or authority to correct it. - Evidence: reviewer task definition, critic task definition, goal progress heuristic. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for task-level reviewer roles; medium-high for absent management audit. - Caveats: User-authored agents could target management verification, but the shipped definitions do not.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Observe prospective external environmental change, evaluate future alternatives and return an adaptive capability plan into current S3 action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New provider offerings, future user requirements, changing codebase dependencies and evolving development workflows. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The root or goal loop pursues present user tasks; future research/model switches or skills are operator-directed or task-local, not first-party external-future strategy selection. - Decision owner: Human for goal/environment/provider selection, coding S1 for task tactics and deterministic goal controls for continuation; no S4 strategist established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional extensions, providers, persistent memory, goal prompts, iterative summary carryover and local code-change progress checks. - Closure path: Existing user goal → repeated code-model iterations informed by previous edits/cost → success sentinel or cap, without external-futures intelligence → adaptation decision → S3 adoption. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Iteration/replanning can improve current code but neither forecasts the outside environment nor governs introduction of new systemic capability. - Evidence: goal-runner iteration, signal and summary, user-provided runtime agent extensions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A future-facing task specified by a user is possible but does not evidence a standing S4 governance function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimately determine final identity, values and policy for the whole coding organization and transmit binding governance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untrusted repository content, risky shell operations, incompatible user directions and potentially harmful code changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human user/project chooses the goal, model, tool scope and approval mode; the package constrains actions but does not acquire legitimate authority to redefine ultimate purpose or values. - Decision owner: Human operator and configured policy/permission checks, not a first-party final-policy agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan/read-only versus auto mode, tool subsets and disallowed tool lists, user instructions, fixed task caps and prompt constraints. - Closure path: User and project policy → session/tool/subagent restrictions → execute/refuse/request approval of particular action; no agent-originated ultimate-governance return to S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model can decide how to perform coding work while governing goals, authorization and identity stay user-supplied. - Evidence: mode overrides in task, per-agent allowed tools, human-created goals and caps. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high regarding externally specified ultimate purpose; no governing self-legitimation claim. - Caveats: auto increases operational discretion but not legitimate S5 authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/875cbb62225236d6fb63bc2a373fbe685188a3cd/assessments/ca/caveman-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 875cbb62225236d6fb63bc2a373fbe685188a3cd\">875cbb6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T18:26:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wulawulu/learn-claude-code-rs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learn-claude-code-rs</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wulawulu/learn-claude-code-rs/tree/2981fc1b6ec3c45843d89412292b6b15505665e3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2981fc1b6ec3c45843d89412292b6b15505665e3\">2981fc1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform coding/investigation outcomes through a continuing observation–decision–tool-action loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown files, implementation alternatives, tool failures, denied actions and incomplete context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select read/edit/shell/delegation actions, interpret their actual result, revise subsequent work and decide when to return. - Decision owner: Model-backed first-party Agent; subagents own their assigned local coding decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider SDK, tool router/context, permission manager, hooks, persistence, compaction and retry logic. - Closure path: User query → Agent request with tools → model-selected action → native/MCP execution → toolresult appended to history → next model decision; task calls return their actual child result to the root. - Boundary reachability: The documented sfull command constructs these tools and calls agentloop directly. task constructs another Agent and awaits that same loop, rather than invoking a separate external coding CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Rust router can enforce or execute actions, but removing model decisions leaves no substantive coding/action choice. User permissions bound the operation rather than select each ordinary step. - Evidence: startup, request/action/result continuation, first-party subagent. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High for source wiring; live effectiveness unmeasured. - Caveats: Requires a compatible configured provider and working tool permissions; source inspection does not establish performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No material inter-S1 conflict-attenuation and returned adjustment loop in the integrated mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate paths are conflicting task ownership, shared files and dependency ordering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Task owner/dependency fields and isolated worktrees do not supply a conflict-specific resolution protocol. - Decision owner: Root chooses task actions; predefined managers update records and enforce local data consistency. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared mutexes, blockedby/blocks metadata, teammate inboxes and named worktree isolation. - Closure path: Synchronous child summaries return, but teammate inbox creation does not feed an executing teammate; worktree results are not reconciled into an inter-worker settlement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic delegation/storage and a theoretical user-written coordination protocol do not meet the S2-specific constructor test. - Evidence: task metadata/updates, team record-only implementation, worktree operations. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High within sfull. - Caveats: Other independently runnable chapters are distinct boundaries; no broader claim about them is made.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-current organizational regulation established beyond local coding-task planning/delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate paths are present task commitments, team plans and shutdown/resource control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The root can edit task state and call a coding child; planapproval/shutdown tools only store a typed message, without a governing control consumer. - Decision owner: Model for current task operation; user/developer for permission/recovery settings. No distinct S3 or parent S3 loop established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task lists, team status records, protocol inboxes, context limits, retries and permission checks. - Closure path: Tool results inform current coding work; no first-party whole-current allocation/accountability or intervention decision returns into a governed worker fleet. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The names manager, team, plan and shutdown do not turn a local tool loop or record API into whole-system present-control ownership. - Evidence: protocol message functions, record persistence, operational prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High within the integrated mode. - Caveats: A developer could compose a manager; a generic toolset is not a supplied S3-specific constructor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No differentiated independent complementary operational audit arrangement in sfull. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect output can be checked by the producing coding agent or a generically delegated coding child. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied claim-relative audit role/independence contract or corrective-control return is assigned. - Decision owner: Producing agent and user in ordinary task completion; developer for arbitrary hook composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: File/shell inspection, hook callbacks, tool-result history and generic child context separation. - Closure path: Tool errors/hooks feed normal production; a user-defined audit is additional composition, not the shipped path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fresh context alone does not supply audit independence. The registered child is a coding agent with write tools, and the standalone s21 evaluator is not integrated here. - Evidence: coding child role/toolset, generic hook interface, integration and chapter boundaries. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High for the bounded integration claim. - Caveats: No claim that tests or an externally composed reviewer lack practical value.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established externally prospective capability-adaptation loop in the integrated coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Context growth, user corrections, remembered project facts and stored future task descriptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The agent chooses task actions and memories; no actor owns prospective environmental options and a return to present capability. - Decision owner: Coding agent for local memory/task work; user for purpose/model settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Memory types, prompt loading, skills, context compaction and cron record CRUD. - Closure path: Memory is re-presented to future prompts and schedules are stored. Neither establishes a prospective adaptation/current-control conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local learning/continuity is not S4, and a persisted cron string does not establish an autonomous strategic worker. - Evidence: memory guidance, cron storage only, prompt/compaction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High within sfull. - Caveats: A coding task may investigate the future; that task content alone does not establish the organizational function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy arbitration and governing return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool risk, user denials and static coding constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human chooses permission mode/action approvals; prompt/configuration supplies operational constraints. - Decision owner: User/developer for execution policy; not an established S5 owner at the session recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Default/Plan/Auto modes, Allow/Deny/Ask, prompt constraints, hooks and CLAUDE.md loading. - Closure path: Approval allows a tool; denial returns to the coding agent. No identity-level matter and returned governing settlement is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission and plan-approval labels do not create ultimate-policy closure; planapproval itself merely appends a message. - Evidence: permission mode and check/ask, tool approval return, static/dynamic prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High for the function distinction. - Caveats: A real user&#39;s higher-level organization may have S5 outside this runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a2e46d2ad2a574c2af216d90a791d791ffcfdd7/assessments/le/learn-claude-code-rs.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a2e46d2ad2a574c2af216d90a791d791ffcfdd7\">9a2e46d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T20:03:23Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Phoenixrr2113/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Phoenix Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Phoenixrr2113/agent-harness/tree/0f67103affd30a3c778874b890dbd5801cdbd94f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0f67103affd30a3c778874b890dbd5801cdbd94f\">0f67103 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce goal-specific model-generated answers and real tool-mediated work, including model-initiated subagent tasks and scheduled workflows. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable user goals and tool outputs, multi-turn reasoning, resource and API failures, tool arguments and dynamic skill selection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose context-dependent actions, tool arguments, next steps and final task output within configured tool/provider/guardrail limits. - Decision owner: The configured primary LLM through first-party createHarness/Conversation; delegated child model owns its own bounded task output, but the parent chooses whether to invoke it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System context loader, primary model API, model-driven tools and MCP, permission wrapper, session store and optional scheduler. - Closure path: Request or scheduled prompt → model interprets loaded identity/rules and selects an action → allowed tool executes and returns evidence → model updates its response → final result/session saved for subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: Shipped CLI supports harness run/chat and the first-party createHarness path wires actual provider inference to tools, sessions and state. Provider credentials/availability remain external requirements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive local responses and tool actions; deterministic infrastructure only bounds, logs and executes the selected operations. - Evidence: model setup/boot, model run with tools, saved outputs and feedback, child-agent model invocation, Conversation flow. - Basis: Source-explicit model-backed agent/tool loop reachable from shipped CLI. - Confidence: High structural, not externally benchmarked at this review ref. - Caveats: No credential-backed provider/tool success was executed as part of the assessment; unit/integration test successes are not inference-quality evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Regulate interference and oscillation between distinct S1 operating units without simply centralizing every decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential overlapping file/working-state effects when separately invoked scheduled work, parent and delegated agent tasks share the local harness directory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identifiable shipped decisive inter-worker collision-resolution or mutually negotiated scope/budget adjustment with documented changed downstream S1 behavior. - Decision owner: Not established for this function at the declared recursion; a parent model&#39;s tool selection and static scheduler control are not independent S2 coordination decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Generic delegateTo task calls, scheduler cooldown and no-recursion child tool configuration, per-file lock helpers, local session state. - Closure path: Message/task routing and file serialization helpers are present, but no demonstrated inter-S1 disturbance → coordination decision → affected S1 rescheduling/repartition outcome transaction closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The primary agent can delegate one task; that alone is decomposition, not S2. The file-lock primitive&#39;s withFileLock proceeds even when lock acquisition fails, so it is not source evidence of fail-closed coordination among independent S1s; no first-party consumer transaction using that helper was found. - Evidence: stateless child invocation, parent agent tools, scheduler cooldown, lock helpers&#39; fail-open behavior. - Basis: Bounded negative; declared S1 plurality alone is insufficient. - Confidence: Medium-high for reviewed standard runtime; no blanket claim against adopter-written plugins. - Caveats: Separate user-configured scripts could implement specific inter-worker conflict attenuation beyond the reviewed default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Maintain the whole resident agent cell&#39;s current viable operation within configured spending and request limits across repeated runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Aggregated spending and request-rate growth across sessions and scheduled/current prompts that would exceed user-authorized resource ceilings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Enforce a configured stop/allow decision at the cell&#39;s primary model-run entry point using live usage records and daily/monthly cap policy. - Decision owner: Current operating envelopes are chosen by the user/configuration; first-party deterministic checkGuardrails implements that narrowly scoped management constructor. There is no resident model selecting or renegotiating the budgets. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted cost records, rate limiter, daily/monthly spending aggregation, current health state and primary run/stream preflight. - Closure path: Current primary-run request → shared cost/rate records are assessed → configured limit leads to block or permit → next model work is prevented or allowed and usage is recorded for future decisions. - Boundary reachability: createHarness.run and its streaming counterpart call the guardrail directly before model generation in shipped primary runtime. A configured spending cap denies execution on exceeded usage, rather than merely rendering a dashboard. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C is the delivered deterministic current resource-control constructor; no discretionary S3=A is asserted. This does not prove complete whole-of-project coordination/priority optimization. - Whole-system current view: Daily/monthly totals and time-window request counts across this configured resident agent&#39;s recorded primary sessions; includes multiple user and scheduled invocations, not company-wide organizational resources. - Current-control decision scope: Whether new primary model work proceeds within quota, with static owner-configured limits; subordinate delegate paths may bypass this particular check and are not claimed fully covered. - Evidence: shared budget and rate guard logic, entry blocking and exception path, health success/failure tracking, scheduler running workflows. - Basis: Specific instantiated current-resource controller with feedback and execution effect. - Confidence: Medium: narrow constructor demonstrated, management autonomy not. - Caveats: This is not a portfolio manager; nested agent calls can use direct generate rather than all going through the same top-level gate. A static limit would not justify S3=A, and additional source-owned budget negotiation is not shown.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently examine raw operational truth beyond ordinary S1 reports and return findings capable of correcting S3/worker decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model task failure, falsely claimed successful outputs or misleading quality/improvement claims needing independent correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No default first-party independent raw-evidence operational auditor with binding corrective findings returned to current control is established. - Decision owner: None in the normal run/learn/approval loop; user may run optional skill evaluations but these do not install independent review of normal agent claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session logs, health diagnostics, doctor rules, tool approvals, verification criteria, optional skill eval-quality/trigger tests and rules promote. - Closure path: Operational outputs are logged, and separate optional skill benchmarks can run, but no independently empowered auditor is automatically fed task-primary evidence and returns a mandatory corrective finding into the current work controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A real opt-in with/without-skill benchmark is useful evaluation, yet is not a second operational inspection channel with corrective S3 authority over the live goal. The nominal rules promote gate&#39;s installed adapter returns fixed optimistic pass signals rather than observed pass/fail samples; it cannot be credited as independent quality audit. - Evidence: quality eval actually compares outputs, optional optimize quality approval, rule promotion gating API, actual promoter fixed success signals, verification gate checks, approval asks user, not independent evidence. - Basis: Bounded negative for operational S3 in the shipped resident-agent standard mode. - Confidence: Medium-high; no claim that all independent plugins are impossible. - Caveats: Isolated skill benchmark assertions or third-party independent reviewer plugins may augment the system, but cannot be imported as demonstrated default audit ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Model prospective external changes/opportunities, propose viable future capability adjustments and return those options for present adoption. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in user requirements, outside tooling/competitors, model capabilities or future environmental conditions requiring new skills or organizational capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established: the journal model extracts retrospective repeated behaviours from completed sessions, and optional quality optimizer suggests changes against existing skill-eval cases, not possible future outside environments. - Decision owner: No source-owned externally prospective options manager or returned S3 adoption choice. The user chooses new skills/provider and installed rules are based on past local interaction evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session capture, model-generated journal, instinct proposals, scheduled auto-learn/auto-install, optional skill optimize and future prompt/rule reload. - Closure path: A real past-interaction → future prompt-modification mechanism exists, but there is no sensed external future alternatives → option model → current S3 capability decision and feedback path. rules promote cannot supply missing strategic selection via its stubbed evaluations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model judgment over recurrent session text changes later behaviour, but generic self-learning/prompt tuning is not S4 unless externally and prospectively oriented. - Evidence: journal text comes from past sessions, LLM derives instincts from past events, optional learned rule installation, scheduled auto learning, rule use on subsequent boots, quality optimization inputs. - Basis: Bounded negative despite actual agent-derived persistent learning. - Confidence: Medium-high for this reviewed revision and stated resident-agent boundary. - Caveats: Real cross-session prompt evolution is acknowledged as operational adaptation; user-defined prospective skills can be authored but are not shipped as a default closed organizational S4 mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate ultimate purpose and identity governance, including resolution when current operations and future adaptation pull in opposite directions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting long-lived agent purpose, changes in acceptable work domain, ultimate owner authorization and identity-level tradeoffs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party live route to an ultimate identity arbiter and binding return path is established. - Decision owner: End user/operator authors IDENTITY.md, limits, approval choices and configuration from outside the agent cell; model/system prompt restrictions do not grant a legitimate autonomous S5 right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: IDENTITY.md, rule files, config, approval on selected tools, content filters and static safety/permission guards. - Closure path: Local prompts/approvals govern particular tool/run choices; no constitutional-level identity conflict → legitimate ultimate authority deliberation → durable binding mission revision path is source-evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent reading an identity file does not make it the legitimate governor of that identity, and ordinary [y/n] tool approval is not a parent-governed S5 return loop. - Evidence: identity/rules loaded into system, human tool approval path, guardrail config, content filter execution. - Basis: Bounded negative at first-party user-installed agent recursion. - Confidence: High for standard modes. - Caveats: A human/adopter organization can supply separate governing S5, but none is wired as a credible first-party identity-level return path in this revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e35080631942e25a18babc4420c7ef285c4c1da5/assessments/ph/phoenix-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e35080631942e25a18babc4420c7ef285c4c1da5\">e350806 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T21:08:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OTT-Cybersecurity-LLC/lyrie-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lyrie</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OTT-Cybersecurity-LLC/lyrie-ai/tree/96048f6727c01760850e5ef5e1526c8cb9061db8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 96048f6727c01760850e5ef5e1526c8cb9061db8\">96048f6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform bounded autonomous model-directed user/security tasks with actual tool/environment observations returned to the model. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown security/tool requests, differing repository/website states, uncertain code context, command failures, changing data and optional concurrent child work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party LyrieEngine accepts user task, model decides next built-in tool and arguments conditioned on previous actual Shield-gated result; optional model choice of spawnsubagent produces independently executed worker result, then parent model continues. - Decision owner: Configured model acting through first-party LyrieEngine/ToolExecutor and separate first-party isolated subagent runner, rather than an externally authored ACP agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ModelRouter, Shield input/tool and untrusted output checks, memory retrieval, tool registry and tool execution, max turns/max tool calls, subagent context/time limits. - Closure path: user/source task → first-party model/tool decision → real tool (or model-spawned subagent) execution → observation/error returned as tool role → model revises next action/final result. - Boundary reachability: scripts/start-all.ts constructs real LyrieEngine, LyrieEngine.initialize registers tools including spawnsubagent, and process directly invokes ToolExecutor.execute in response to model actions. Documented lyrie hack is another static security task operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive tools/next steps dynamically; Lyrie owns implementation of loop and action-result feedback. Defensive tool rules constrain, but do not themselves choose the semantic task. - Evidence: packages/core/src/engine/lyrie-engine.ts; packages/core/src/tools/tool-executor.ts; packages/core/src/tools/spawn-subagent.ts; scripts/start-all.ts. - Basis: Explicit first-party reachable model/tool loop. - Confidence: High for code-level path; no actual provider model or pentest task run attempted. - Caveats: Model API keys and appropriate target authorization required. Prompt-only safety shouldn&#39;t be construed as a universal complete authorization system.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No specific first-party function demonstrably corrects interfering or oscillating behavior between distinct autonomous operating units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Spawning too many subagent tasks can exceed configured capacity and messages may contain untrusted text, but those are general throughput and content-safety constraints rather than a witnessed cross-agent collision/oscillation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Subagent concurrency limit rejects new tasks, in-process bus routes Shield-filtered messages, and parent awaits/collects results; no interference-sensitive sibling adjustment judgment is evidenced. - Decision owner: Static SubAgentManager limits and transport bus, with parent agent supplying task instructions but not a demonstrated S2 correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxConcurrent, maxTurns, timeout/abort, results Map, message-bus subscriber/recipient routes, Shield filtering. - Closure path: Model chooses spawn → capacity admission/error/result → same parent&#39;s task continuation; not conflict between two S1 actions → coordination selection → modified sibling actions. - Distinct S1 units: Separately model-driven child loops are real when model-spawned; the configured manager can run several. Their concurrency alone is not S2. - Inter-S1 disturbance: No source-specific competing target/worker write collision, oscillation or actual resource conflict addressed with mutual adaptive behavior, only fixed process admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An allowed maximum parallel count, a shared A2A bus and a task aggregation call don&#39;t independently prove S2 function or owner. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agents/sub-agent.ts; packages/core/src/agents/message-bus.ts; packages/core/src/agents/subagent-runner.ts. - Basis: Bounded negative search on strongest coordination candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high for standard current code. - Caveats: Separate external users can organize worker teams, but their coordination isn&#39;t automatically first-party.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current manager of independently ongoing operations with system-wide prioritization, commitments and exception intervention was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long tasks, phase failures, accumulated child results and scan findings are regulated locally by S1 task loops and deterministic stage scripts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model may choose to spawn or await child tasks, while HackOrchestrator always executes a fixed ordered security assessment workflow. Neither demonstrates live regulation of competing current operational work commitments across the whole deployment. - Decision owner: Within-task Lyrie model and static script; no separate installed whole-current manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LyrieCoordinator prompt/whitelist (not activated in LyrieEngine), model router, task count limits, HackOrchestrator phase/scan report and daemon tick callbacks. - Closure path: A child&#39;s output affects its parent&#39;s same task and scripted phases advance to report. No whole-installation current state → authorized resource/priority intervention → redistributed operating commitments feedback path identified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Coordinator-labelled static constants lack runtime ownership, and the task&#39;s own next-step selection is S1 rather than Beer S3. The daemon schedules tick callback but doesn&#39;t own current cross-S1 resource arbitration. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agents/coordinator.ts; packages/core/src/engine/lyrie-engine.ts; packages/core/src/hack/orchestrator.ts; packages/core/src/engine/daemon.ts. - Basis: Bounded negative of supplied first-party whole-now decision paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A different deployment using custom orchestration might implement current work management atop these APIs; not part of pinned standard configuration.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Challenge scanner-proposed vulnerability findings using distinct attack-surface/trust-boundary, structural reachability and code-path facts and feed corrections into reported and remediated claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False-positive syntactic scanner matches, findings in tests/comments/dead code, missing reachability evidence and unjustified severity or unsupported confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Static Stages A–D re-evaluate raw scanner claims using separate source/reachability/trust-boundary context, downgrade/deny confirmation and select which claims pass through to remediations and final security report. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic validateBatch and precomputed separate AttackSurface evidence; there is no independently deciding model or human reviewer in the lyrie hack path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RawFinding annotations, shield pattern reality checks, AttackSurface map/flow context, staged StageVerdict labels, severity/confidence thresholds, PoC snippet/human precondition and dedup logic. - Closure path: Raw scanner finding → alternative trust-boundary/reachability/source code context → A/B/C/D validator sets confirmed/severity/confidence → HackOrchestrator only generates remediations from confirmed and serializes filtered/confirmed lists into actual report. - Claim being audited: Whether a scanner-emitted potential vulnerability is supported as a confirmed/reachable security issue worth remediation. - Ordinary reporting path: The scanning engine&#39;s raw findings and reported severity. - Complementary access path: Independently built first-party AttackSurface representation and deterministic code-path/test-context heuristics, rather than simply accepting scanner label. - Independence boundary: Different analysis rules and extra context within same process; no independent model, live PoC proof by default, tamper-proof scanner provenance or detached human verifier. Findings with incomplete evidence can slip through. - Who acts on findings: HackOrchestrator.run consumes validated, creates suggested fixes only for confirmed findings and prepares JSON/SARIF/Markdown reports. Stage F auto-fix/PoC is not certified execution. - Boundary reachability: lyrie hack command&#39;s HackOrchestrator.run calls validateBatch(rawFindings,{surface,shield,fastMode}); the resulting confirmed flags actually influence remediation/report state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic complementary adjudication owns the challenge and return; LyrieVerifier class prompts cannot be given credit as a live autonomous audit where normal process never instantiates it. Thus S3=C. - Evidence: packages/core/src/hack/orchestrator.ts; packages/core/src/pentest/stages-validator.ts; packages/core/src/pentest/attack-surface.ts; packages/core/src/agents/verifier.ts. - Basis: Source-reachable static alternative-evidence adjudication, bounded coverage. - Confidence: Medium; stage verification is weaker than independent artifact replay or secondary agent critique. - Caveats: Part of routine security-pipeline gating; doesn&#39;t establish independent reviewer deployment. Some stages use metadata provided by same RawFinding source rather than independently rereading repository content.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No actual first-party future-oriented organizational capability-adaptation loop closed from external environment feedback through selected options into the installed agent&#39;s later operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Outcome quality, evolving threats, adversarial target prompts and missed skills provide improvement clues but are not in themselves organizational prospective adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Manual lyrie evolve dream invokes a heuristic outcome-to-markdown skill extractor and pruning, while engine post-task checkForImprovement is supplied an empty step list and always returns null. The extracted ~/.lyrie/evolve/skills/.md aren&#39;t the ~/.lyrie/skills/.json objects loaded by SkillManager. No real autonomous proposal/admission into future operational skill selection is shown. - Decision owner: Operator invokes batch, deterministic score/prune rules and optional custom LLM interface that isn&#39;t the shipped default skill-content chooser. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DreamCycle, SkillExtractor heuristic fallback, scored task outcomes, SKILL.md files, cosine duplicate checks, training-data exporter; separate per-task adversarial corpus mutation. - Closure path: Scored prior outcomes → written markdown skill candidates and report. Missing: admitted skill consumed by default SkillManager → changed later autonomous tool/strategy choice or a feedback loop with current management. - External distinction: Threat feeds and target responses are observed in current research; no demonstrated environmental option tracking beyond task-level data. - Future / prospective distinction: Dream is named as future skill improvement, but no installed skill-adoption/selection link into runtime was found. - Adaptation option generated: Heuristic summaries/markdown patterns in a separate optional batch directory, or operator-selected train exporter output, not first-party model-chosen future strategy installed in the active engine. - Path back into current capability / S3: No verified import from evolving markdown directory into active JSON skill registry or model/tool next-run control; developers could wire it later, not creditable now. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Default heuristic summaries and file creation do not constitute an autonomous model selecting tested future capability change. AAV mutation changes a target test stimulus, not Lyrie&#39;s enduring organizational skill. - Evidence: packages/core/src/engine/lyrie-engine.ts; packages/core/src/skills/skill-manager.ts; packages/core/src/evolve/dream-cycle.ts; packages/core/src/evolve/skill-extractor.ts; scripts/evolve.ts; packages/core/src/aav/adversarial-loop.ts. - Basis: Bounded negative investigation of stronger reachable model improvement candidates and missing operational return. - Confidence: Medium-high for standard run; future integrations could close the path. - Caveats: An operator can manually consume generated markdown or train another model externally; that is a different operating mode and doesn&#39;t establish an installed first-party S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separate ultimate purpose/ethos/policy authority or conflict settlement loop established for the deployed agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool permissions, credentials, unauthorized commands, untrusted output and agent identity/scope revocation are security concerns governed by preconfigured trust rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator/project maintainers configure Shield/permissions/trust certs, and deterministic signed ATP verifies scope/revocation and permission gates. No actor changes ultimate mission/identity under a legitimate higher-level policy decision. - Decision owner: External operator/organization for policy selection; static cryptographic and Shield primitives for enforcing choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ATP identity certificates, receipts, scope permissions, revocation/kill-switch, Shield scans, risk classes, memory integrity and governance scorecard. - Closure path: Static identity/allowed scope → validate request/tool/attestation and optionally block. No first-party ultimate value dispute → policy judgment → S3/S4 return or identity revision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cryptographic identity/safety enforcement isn&#39;t a live S5 policy holder, even if sophisticated or branded &#39;agent trust protocol&#39;. - Evidence: packages/atp/src/verify.ts; packages/atp/src/scope.ts; packages/atp/src/kill-switch.ts; packages/core/src/governance/permissions.ts; packages/core/src/engine/shield-manager.ts. - Basis: Bounded negative of active policy/identity paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The real parent security organization may provide S5 outside this standalone OSS runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/eeb55fc7285f749dae8712747df0e64e396549df/assessments/lyrie.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit eeb55fc7285f749dae8712747df0e64e396549df\">eeb55fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T15:21:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/onevcat/argue\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">argue</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/onevcat/argue/tree/7939e67c8479aa3b9f5d070f5e7feff8b90f876d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7939e67c8479aa3b9f5d070f5e7feff8b90f876d\">7939e67 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Generate independent argument, critique, claim refinement and votes for a user-directed deliberative outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended user questions, competing interpretations, prior participant arguments, claim disagreements and unpredictable model responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each configured model participant decides its proposed arguments, whether a claim is accepted/rejected/revised/merged and its final vote; subsequent prompts incorporate its output and peers&#39; outputs. - Decision owner: Shipped, configured first-party participant task loop invoking actual API/SDK/CLI model actors. The model provider executes inference, but argue assembles and operationally invokes the distributed deliberation actors and routes their judgments to subsequent phases. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Zod input/output contracts, per-participant sticky sessions, model adapters, structured prompts, claim IDs, round budgets, waiting policies and result storage. - Closure path: User task and configured model participants → executeHeadlessRun → ArgueEngine.start → real taskDelegate.dispatch/provider inference → round outputs and peer feedback → next-round judgment/final vote → consensus/report/result delivered to caller. - Boundary reachability: The standard CLI connects the concrete ArgueEngine to createTaskDelegate; real provider runners, not only abstract host interfaces, execute model prompts. The library&#39;s uninstantiated delegate alone is insufficient, but the installed CLI mode closes it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the invoked model participants and there is no discretionary argument, critique or vote, only a deterministic schema/wait/score pipeline. Agent content choices are the decisive operational judgments, even if the model weights and vendor implementation are external. - Evidence: wired headless runtime; dispatch; API model call; debate prompt. - Basis: Structural first-party standard-mode loop; no runtime/model-quality experiment. - Confidence: High for source-supported loop assembly, no claim about live response quality. - Caveats: Provider SDKs/external coding CLI agents are dependencies; their internal task rights and governance are not argue-owned. CLI mock does not establish autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: A narrowly exposed deliberation-coordination constructor reconciles incompatible contributions from separately configured S1 participant work cells into a usable collective claim set. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Independent participant answers can contradict one another, duplicate factual claims or repeatedly reintroduce already-merged propositions, destabilizing the coherence of the group&#39;s shared response. This is a concrete cross-participant consistency conflict, not mere process concurrency. - Distinct S1 units: At least two separately configured and invoked model participants, each with a distinct participantId, role, sticky session, output and per-round model call; their generated answers supply the competing contributions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: An output&#39;s judgements may disagree with another participant&#39;s active claim or propose mergesWith for a duplicate; redundant claim IDs and inconsistent statements would otherwise persist into future rounds. - Attenuating coordination relation: The first-party updateClaims merges duplicates, remaps references to the surviving claim, accepts revision suggestions, limits visible claims to active items, and republishes bounded peer responses plus the shared revised catalog to every subsequent participant round. Final individual claim votes are reduced by a configured consensus threshold. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: runRound builds each later participant prompt with updated claimCatalog and opposing peerRoundInputs; previously merged claims are excluded from active catalog and debate prompts explicitly forbid reopening already merged claims. A model&#39;s next judgment therefore receives the resolved shared distinctions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The first-party debate engine supplies the reconciliation and vote feedback path; model participants choose substantive critiques/merge proposals, while the host selects roles, number of rounds, policies and consensus threshold. It does not install an autonomous general-purpose inter-agent conflict manager. - Decision owner: A function-specific first-party constructor, closed for the bounded discussion mode but with coordination policy/authority still composed by the host. Content autonomy belongs to participant S1s; deterministic merge and threshold application do not transfer general S2 policy ownership to a model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared claim ID catalog, round feedback payload, DefaultWaitCoordinator, active participant set, timeout/cancel, bounded peer context, merged claim graph and fixed early-stop threshold. - Closure path: Two or more agents emit conflicting claims → S2-specific claim/judgment reducer normalizes revisions and merges → catalog plus peer objections return in next-round prompts → agents can revise and final-vote → bounded consensus/disagreements returned in the result. - Boundary reachability: Shipped argue-cli wires real provider delegates to ArgueEngine.start; its runRound repeatedly invokes updateClaims and repackages the new catalog/peer objections before final voting. This is active in the normal multi-participant deliberation path, not just a developer example or generic queue. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The code specifically recognizes disagreement and semantic overlap as a conflict, transmits opposing arguments for revision and prevents merged claims from oscillating back into the active dispute. Mere parallel dispatch, timeout or participant identity would not establish this witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model actors choose substantive merge/critique proposals, but removing a hypothetical independent S2 agent leaves the same developer-defined reconciliation protocol, prompt cadence and threshold. The shipped construct is C, not fully autonomous S2 A. - Evidence: distinct round task dispatch and peer input; claim merge and canonicalization; debate anti-reopening prompts; final resolution votes. - Basis: Structural first-party cross-participant disagreement/reconciliation witness; narrow exposed S2 constructor with real CLI wiring. - Confidence: Medium-high for the feedback path; no evidence that models reach factual correctness or agreement empirically. - Caveats: This S2 claim concerns contradictions between discussion contributors, not runtime tool/resource contention across unrelated agent work. The first-party method resolves claim identity/revisions mechanically; autonomous organizational conflict-policy ownership is not established.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party global present resource/commitment management of independently operating units at the service level beyond fixed single-request debate rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed participants, elapsed deadlines, differing scores and incomplete votes; these are session-level outcome constraints, not proven whole-service commitments requiring S3 discretion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The engine deterministically eliminates failed/timed-out participants, computes scores and chooses the representative final speaker. It does not review/reallocate overall ongoing organizational priorities or resource commitments based on a whole-current operational view. - Decision owner: Fixed runtime policy from the host&#39;s start request; no installed S3 agent or composed S3-specific delegated judgment. Selecting a final report voice is a product output decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session store, metrics, state machine, minParticipants, per-task timeout, scoring tie-break, explicit representative configuration. - Closure path: Round failure removes one debate participant, and scores choose final speech/report composer. No observed whole-service current-control decision changes multiple operations or allocates scarce resources under a global manager. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic score and failover rules regulate one debate run. They do not establish autonomous S3 ownership or an independently composed S3 parent-control mode. - Evidence: participant lifecycle / score / representative; scoring; timeout. - Basis: Bounded negative, excluding mere fixed workflow/selection from S3. - Confidence: Medium-high for this one-request recursion. - Caveats: A calling institution may exercise its own S3 over repeated deliberation runs, but that actor is not automatically a built-in argue metasystem.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently sourced inspection of operational facts complementing routine participant claims and returning corrective evidence to an S3 current-control owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect participant arguments or votes may degrade the result, but ordinary debate challenge and score calculations reuse participants&#39; own text/judgments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Participants critique each other and the engine scores them using peer agreement and text-derived metrics; no separate audit authority samples external task truth or replays actual independently observed operational results. - Decision owner: Regular debate participants and deterministic scoring/report code; no first-party complementary auditor with independent access to the operational environment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Full round record, structured claims, disagreements, final votes, JSONL events and stored report. - Closure path: Peer critiques become ordinary next-round input and vote totals become report content; no independent audit finding is routed to a distinct current-control correction function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Peer disagreement is intentionally the S1 product; a consensus score or review prompt is not by itself complementary direct access to reality. - Evidence: peer-review correctness from participants&#39; votes; round reports/votes; headless observer. - Basis: Bounded negative despite nontrivial first-party critique and traceability. - Confidence: Medium-high for shipped operating mode. - Caveats: A host can supply evidence to a topic or build an external validator, but no distinct shipped audit-to-control path is established by doing so.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No installed external/future sensing, capability-adaptation option selection and return to present organizational operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user needs or model reliability might motivate future adaptation, but the engine instead performs the one requested deliberation with preconfigured modes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User chooses models/policy and debate topic; participants reason about that topic. They do not choose and install future capability changes for argue. - Decision owner: Host/operator outside the assessed loop for configuration and upgrades; current model participants for task content. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API/provider configuration, round policies, participant roles, debate history, result artifacts. - Closure path: Participant thoughts can discuss future topics but terminate as an answer/report/action; no outside prospective observation → adaptation option → S3/present capability update loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Topic exploration or model-driven answer revision within one debate is ordinary S1, not organizational future-intelligence governance. - Evidence: start/run protocol; config-selected provider models; optional action as task. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The software may be embedded in a prospective intelligence service; that downstream application is not shown by this shipped debate CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No shipped ultimate-identity dispute/decision and binding return route at the deliberation service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Role and scoring policy may change the character of an answer, but the service&#39;s identity, legitimate purpose and final authority remain determined by the caller/developer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host chooses topic, roles, model/round policies and optional post-debate action. Participants&#39; threshold votes accept claims, not alter the service&#39;s ultimate purpose or authorization constitution. - Decision owner: User/operator for inputs and runtime settings outside any in-product identity deliberation. No autonomous or parent-owned closed S5 issue escalation/return path is installed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Input schema, constraints field, selected representative, consensus threshold, permission-sensitive external CLI execution options. - Closure path: Votes are returned as a bounded result and may inform a caller; the shipped engine does not turn an identity/policy dispute into an authoritative new rule governing future runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Consensus on a debate claim is not organizational S5. A system-prompt role, fixed constraints or user task approval likewise does not establish ultimate policy authority. - Evidence: input/engine contract; claim consensus criteria; action delegates. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External applications could deliberately deploy argue as a democratic policy procedure, but no normal-mode identity-level return path is demonstrated in this repository.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/87e70a25192f7ca254cce4fd02b9abe2b1f642ab/assessments/ar/argue.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 87e70a25192f7ca254cce4fd02b9abe2b1f642ab\">87e70a2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
